Windows port - unreleased
-------------
This section covers the conversion from Linux to Windows.  It is the record of
what changed for someone using SD; PROJECT_STATUS.md and HISTORY.md in the
repository root carry the reasoning, the state of the work and the traps.
Entries are newest first, by date, until there is a version to attach them to.

03 Sep 26  THE FULL-SCREEN EDITORS NOW COME WITH SD. INSTALLING NO LONGER
           DOWNLOADS THEM.

           The two editors the EDIT and MICRO verbs use - Microsoft Edit and
           micro - used to be fetched from the internet while SD installed.
           They are now part of the installer, and they are put beside SD's
           own programs in

               C:\Program Files\SD\usr\bin

           Three things change for you. The install is quicker, and it no
           longer stops near the end waiting for a download. It works on a
           machine that is offline, behind a proxy, or where the tool that
           did the downloading is blocked or absent - which was the case on
           Windows Server, and on those machines you previously got no MICRO
           at all. And the versions are fixed: micro 2.0.15 and Microsoft
           Edit 1.2.1, which are the versions the editor documentation was
           written from, so the keys it lists are the keys you have.

           EDIT AND MICRO NOW RUN SD'S OWN COPY. They look beside SD first,
           whether or not you ticked "Add SD to PATH" when you installed, and
           fall back to searching your PATH as before. If you already keep
           your own micro or Microsoft Edit somewhere, it is untouched - but
           these two verbs will use SD's, so that a question about a release
           has one answer.

03 Sep 26  COMPILING A PROGRAM WITH AN UNFINISHED "BEGIN TRANSACTION" NO
           LONGER HANGS. IT NOW TELLS YOU WHICH LINE IS WRONG.

           "BEGIN TRANSACTION" opens a block that only "END TRANSACTION"
           closes. If you reached the end of the program without closing it -
           a typo, or a missing line - the compiler did not report the
           mistake. It simply never finished, and there was nothing on screen
           to say why.

           It now reports "Unterminated transaction construct", in about half
           a second, the same way every other unfinished block already did.

           IF YOU HAVE HIT THIS BEFORE, IT MAY HAVE COST YOU MORE THAN THE
           WAIT. A compile that never finishes has to be stopped from
           outside, and doing that could leave the session still counted as
           logged in - which in turn could stop the SD service from shutting
           down cleanly, for a reason that looked unconnected to the program
           you were compiling. That cannot happen from this cause any more.

03 Sep 26  SD NO LONGER TELLS YOU THAT YOU ARE NOT AN ADMINISTRATOR WHEN WHAT
           IT MEANS IS THAT IT COULD NOT FIND OUT.

           SD asks Windows three questions about privilege: is this account an
           administrator, is this session running elevated, and is this user
           allowed to reach the operating system. Each of those checks could
           fail to complete - most often because your account is a domain
           account and the domain controller cannot be reached - and when one
           did, SD treated "I could not find out" as "no".

           The refusal itself was correct and has not changed. SD still
           refuses when it cannot establish that you have the right, which is
           the safe direction. What was wrong was the explanation: an
           administrator in an elevated window could be told to "start the
           shell with Run as administrator", which they had already done, and
           the audit trail recorded "reason=not an administrator" as though
           that had been established.

           SD now says which of the two happened. Where it cannot tell, it
           says so and names the reason, and it writes a line to the error log
           reading "PRIVILEGE CHECK UNDETERMINED". If you are locked out of
           something you believe you have the right to, that line is the one
           to look for: it means the check did not complete, not that your
           rights were found wanting.

03 Sep 26  "list accounts" AND "list os.users" NO LONGER SHOW ACCOUNTS THAT
           HAVE BEEN DELETED.

           A Windows account can be removed without SD being told - "net user
           /delete", a change in Active Directory, a decommissioning script.
           SD kept its own record of that account regardless, so "list
           accounts" and "list os.users" listed accounts that no longer
           existed, and "create.account" refused to make the name again,
           saying "Account already exists" about an account that did not.

           SD now checks its register against Windows every time the SD
           service starts, which is every time the computer starts, and
           removes the records for accounts that have gone. The account's
           directory goes with the record, because deleting an account means
           deleting its data.

           IF YOU WANT TO KEEP AN ACCOUNT'S DATA, SUSPEND IT INSTEAD OF
           DELETING IT: "modify.account <name> suspended" withdraws the
           access and keeps everything else.

           Nothing is removed on a guess. An account of the "GROUP" or
           "OTHER" kind has no Windows login at all, and neither has SDSYS,
           so those are left alone; so is any account whose directory is not
           where SD put it, and any account at all if Windows cannot answer
           the question - for example while a domain controller is
           unreachable. Whatever was left alone, and why, is written to
           "reconcile-accounts.log" beside the database.

03 Sep 26  "remote.api" AND THE INSTALLATION CHECK NOW TELL YOU WHO MAY REACH
           THE API, NOT ONLY THAT IT IS LISTENING.

           A listening API is not a reachable one: the firewall rule can be
           absent, or scoped to this computer. Until now both of these reported
           only the socket, so a machine that could no longer be reached over
           the network still looked fine.

           "remote.api" with no keyword now prints who may reach it as well as
           whether SD opens the port - the same two-part answer "remote.ssh"
           already gives. And "Check the SD installation" adds a line under the
           listening API: whether other computers may reach it, and, when no
           firewall rule admits them, that "remote.api on" opens it.

03 Sep 26  REINSTALLING OVER A DATABASE YOU KEPT NOW TELLS YOU WHAT DID NOT
           COME BACK WITH IT.

           The box that says "YOUR DATA IS UNTOUCHED" was true, and it was
           fair to read it as more than it said. Your data is kept. Who may
           REACH it is not part of your data: uninstalling deletes the Windows
           groups that decide that, and a Windows group takes its membership
           with it.

           So when SD Core was not installed on this computer at the moment
           Setup started, that same box now also says, in the same breath:

             - the ssh-only confinement IS put back, from the account
               register;
             - ssh comes back for everyone in the "sdusers" group, so an
               account whose ssh you had withdrawn can reach this machine
               again;
             - API access does NOT come back, and no install ever grants it.

           And it names the command that sets any of them, per account:

               modify.account <name> ssh | api | both | none

           THE KEYWORD SETS THAT ACCOUNT'S ACCESS TO EXACTLY WHAT IT NAMES.
           "api" on its own takes ssh away; "both" is ssh and the API.

           An ordinary upgrade, over an SD Core that is still installed, loses
           no membership and says none of this.

03 Sep 26  AND THE INSTALLER NOW CHECKS WHETHER ANYTHING CAN ACTUALLY REACH
           THE API, RATHER THAN ONLY WHETHER IT IS RUNNING.

           Uninstalling removes SD Core's own firewall rule for the API port,
           on purpose: leaving a rule behind for a service that is gone would
           be worse. Your sd.conf is kept, so a later reinstall still provides
           the API - and Setup does not ask who may reach it, because that
           setting is yours and it will not overrule you.

           The result was an API that listened and admitted nobody, with
           nothing said about it anywhere. The installer now looks for the
           rule, and when there is none it says so and names the way back:

               remote.api on

           or "remote.api local" for the same rule scoped to this computer
           only. If the rule is there, or SD Core cannot find out, it says
           nothing rather than guessing.

02 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NOW EXPLAINS BOTH REASONS "sd" DOES NOT RUN YET.
           IT USED TO NAME ONLY ONE OF THEM.

           There are two, they look nothing alike, and signing out happens to
           cure both - which is why nobody noticed that the explanation on
           screen matched only one of them.

           A command window that was already open while the installer ran does
           not have the new PATH, and answers that "sd" is not recognized.
           Opening a new window is enough for that one on its own.

           A window opened since has the PATH but still carries the group
           membership you signed in with, and answers that SD Core cannot open
           its files. Only signing out and back in cures that one.

           "Check the SD installation" in the Start Menu now reports the PATH
           as well, and says which of the two you are looking at.

02 Sep 26  listf IN AN ACCOUNT SD CREATED NOW DESCRIBES newvoc, INSTEAD OF
           SHOWING A BARE "F" FOR IT.

           Every other file in the listing carried its description. This one
           record was missing the text in the copy new accounts are built
           from, so it was the only one that came out bare.

02 Sep 26  THE VERB IS NOW update.accounts, NOT update.account. IT ALWAYS
           UPDATED MORE THAN ONE.

           Run in SDSYS as an administrator it refreshes SDSYS and then offers
           to refresh every registered account, which is what it was doing
           under the old name. The plural says so.

           YOU NO LONGER NEED TO VISIT EACH ACCOUNT. The closing page used to
           say "sign in and run this once in each account". That was never
           necessary, and it could not be done at all in a STANDARD or
           PROGRAMMER account, because the verb belongs to administrators. One
           run in SDSYS, answering Y, does all of them.

02 Sep 26  YOU CAN NOW PROTECT A VOC RECORD YOU HAVE CHANGED. PUT [locked] IN
           FIELD 1 AND update.accounts WILL LEAVE IT ALONE.

           An upgrade refreshes each account's VOC from the shipped one, which
           is how you get commands added by a new release. Until now that also
           meant a VOC record you had customised was replaced, with nothing
           said and no way to prevent it.

           Add [locked] anywhere in field 1 of the record, AFTER the type code,
           and update.accounts will never replace it. The first character of
           field 1 is the type code itself - two for a P - so [locked] must not
           come first: write "V [locked]" and not "[locked]V". Upper or lower
           case both work.

           THE RECORD THEN KEEPS THE VERSION YOU HAVE, INCLUDING ANY FAULT WE
           HAVE SINCE CORRECTED IN IT. That is the point of the lock and it is
           your decision, so update.accounts tells you which records it skipped
           every time it runs. Remove [locked] and run it again to take the new
           version.

           VERBS ARE THE ONE EXCEPTION: A LOCKED VERB IS UPDATED ANYWAY, AND
           YOU ARE TOLD. A verb is what SD runs, so holding an old one back
           would leave your account calling a program, or an internal routine,
           that the release has changed - and for the verbs dispatched by
           number that goes wrong silently rather than loudly. The lock is
           honoured on every other kind of record.

           IF YOU NEED A VERB TO BEHAVE DIFFERENTLY, ADD YOUR OWN. Give it a
           name SD does not ship and model it on the system one. update.accounts
           only ever visits records SD ships, so a verb of your own is never
           touched and needs no [locked] marker.

02 Sep 26  ACCOUNTS SD CREATES NOW KEEP THE DESCRIPTIONS IN THEIR VOC RECORDS.

           "listf" in the SDSYS account shows a description beside every file -
           "File - Account register", "File - BASIC program source". In an
           account created with create.account it showed a bare "F" instead,
           because the descriptions were being stripped as the records were
           copied in. Keyword records lost a second description the same way.

           They are kept now. An account created from this release on will read
           the way SDSYS always has. Existing accounts pick the descriptions up
           the next time update.accounts runs in them.

02 Sep 26  SD NO LONGER OFFERS TO SET "NO PASSWORD FOR NOW". A PASSWORD IS
           REQUIRED, AND THE MESSAGES NOW SAY SO.

           The password prompt used to offer an empty line as a way to skip the
           step, and said the account would still work at this computer from an
           administrator session - just not over ssh or the API. That account
           could never exist. SD asks for a password every time you sign in
           until one is set, and ends the session if you do not give one, so
           there was no way to reach it.

           Nothing about what SD does has changed: the empty line ended the
           session before and still does. What changed is that the prompt, the
           refusal, and the installer's closing page now describe that instead
           of describing something else. Until an account has a password it
           cannot be used at all - not at the keyboard, not over ssh, and not
           through the SD API.

01 Sep 26  ssh.server install NOW WARNS ABOUT THE DOWNLOAD TIME AND ASKS BEFORE
           IT STARTS.

           Installing the OpenSSH server pulls it from Windows Update, which can
           take from a few minutes to about an hour with little on screen. The
           command now says so and waits for a yes before starting, so it can be
           aborted rather than appearing to hang. (ssh.server remove already
           confirmed; now install does too.)

01 Sep 26  create.account / modify.account NOW WARN WHEN AN ACCOUNT HAS NO WAY
           TO SIGN IN YET.

           If you set an account up to sign in over ssh but no ssh server is
           installed, or over the API but no API listener is running, the
           command now says so and names how to turn that transport on. It is a
           heads-up, not a refusal: the access is already granted and takes
           effect the moment you install the ssh server or turn on the API, with
           nothing more to do to the account.

01 Sep 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER INSTALLS THE OpenSSH SERVER BY DEFAULT.

           Installing the OpenSSH server is now an unticked choice in the
           installer rather than something done automatically.  Ticking it
           still installs it; leaving it unticked skips a download Windows
           fetches from Windows Update, which is a few minutes on a fast
           machine and up to about an hour on a slow one.

           This does not stop you creating or using SD Core accounts.  An
           account signs in over ssh once a server is installed, or through the
           SD Core API if you provide that instead; and an administrator uses
           SD Core by typing "sd" at an elevated prompt either way.  If you skip
           the server now, install it later by running the installer again and
           ticking the box.

01 Sep 26  delete.file NO LONGER REPORTS A FAILED INDEX DELETE ON A FILE THAT
           NEVER HAD AN INDEX.

           Deleting an ordinary file printed "Failed to delete index directory"
           and set an error return code, even though the delete had completely
           succeeded.  A file that really did have an index deleted quietly and
           correctly, so the message appeared on exactly the files it did not
           apply to.

           SD was asking where the index directory would live, getting an
           answer for every file, and then treating "there was nothing there to
           delete" as "the delete was refused".

           It now checks whether the directory is actually there first, and
           reports a failure only when one exists and cannot be removed.  A
           delete that worked no longer hands a script an error code.

01 Sep 26  A FAILURE WHILE EXTENDING AN INDEX NO LONGER DAMAGES THE INDEX.

           When SD needed a new block for an alternate key index and could not
           get one - a disk error, a full disk, a file held open by something
           else - it carried on and used block zero.  Block zero is the index
           header.  The write went over it, and the index was then permanently
           damaged: every select, list or query on that key read from a header
           that had a data block written on top of it.

           The error was always reported, but only after the damage was done,
           so the message named a temporary problem and left a permanent one.

           SD now stops the operation and reports the error before writing
           anything.  The index is left as it was.

           A healthy disk never reached this, and nothing about normal indexing
           changes.  If you have an index that has been behaving oddly after a
           disk problem, rebuild it.

01 Sep 26  A DELETE THAT FAILS INSIDE A TRANSACTION IS NO LONGER REPORTED AS
           DONE.

           Deleting a record from a directory file inside a transaction did not
           check whether the delete actually happened.  If the file was
           read-only, locked by another program, or otherwise protected, the
           commit reported success and the record stayed on disk - so a later
           read, select or list returned a record you had been told was gone.

           It now reports the error, the same way a delete outside a
           transaction always has.

01 Sep 26  DELETING AN ACCOUNT NOW WARNS THAT THE DATA GOES TOO, AND OFFERS
           SUSPENDING INSTEAD.

           The confirmation named what would be removed - the account, its
           directory, the Windows login - but not that everything the account
           holds goes with them, and it did not mention that there is another
           way.

           It now says both, before it asks.  The question and its default
           answer of "no" are unchanged.

           If you only want to stop somebody using SD, suspend the account
           instead.  Suspending keeps everything.

01 Sep 26  SUSPENDING AN ACCOUNT NOW SAYS WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.

           A suspended account loses all access to SD - local login, ssh, the
           API and logto.  Nothing at the Windows level changes: the Windows
           account, its password and its group memberships are all left alone,
           including administrator rights.

           That is correct and has not changed.  What is new is that suspending
           says so at the time, rather than leaving an administrator to assume
           one way or the other.  It also says how to lift it.

01 Sep 26  AN EMPTY DIRECTORY NAMED AFTER A DRIVE LETTER IS NO LONGER LEFT IN
           THE DATA TREE.

           Creating an account left a stray, empty directory beside the system
           files, named "C:" after the drive letter of the account's own path.
           It appeared once per installation, on the first account created,
           and nothing used it.

           It was hard to see, which is why it lasted: a colon cannot appear
           in a Windows file name, so anything that asked for this directory
           by name was told it did not exist.  Listing the folder showed it.

           An existing one is not removed by the upgrade, only never made
           again.  Deleting it by hand is safe.

01 Sep 26  THE AUDIT TRAIL NO LONGER RECORDS AN ELEVATION THAT DID NOT HAPPEN.

           When an administrator signs in and is put into SDSYS, the trail
           recorded "ELEVATION GRANTED" at the point the elevation was
           obtained - which is well before the rights are actually given, and
           two things can still refuse the login in between: SDSYS being
           suspended, and its directory being unreadable.

           Either of those produced a trail carrying both "ELEVATION GRANTED"
           and "LOGIN REFUSED" for the same session.  The record contradicted
           itself, and the false half was the half about privilege.

           The record is now written after the rights exist, so it cannot
           disagree with them.  If granting them does not work the login is
           refused rather than continuing as though it had.

           A refused login whose account directory could not be entered also
           records why, instead of "reason=unspecified".

01 Sep 26  A TIER CHANGE NO LONGER REPORTS A REMOVAL THAT DID NOT HAPPEN.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT removes things when an account moves down a tier:
           the VOC records the higher tier had, and the os.users record an
           administrator gets.  Both removals were reported as done without
           anything checking that they had been.

           So a VOC record that could not be deleted was still counted in
           "VOC: n records added, n removed, n left alone", leaving the
           account holding a command it should have lost while being told the
           tier had changed.  And a grant record that could not be deleted
           still produced "os.users: the record for X is removed".

           Both now report what actually happened.  A record that could not be
           removed is counted separately and named - it is not folded into
           "left alone", which means something else - and the os.users failure
           says so instead of claiming the record went.

01 Sep 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW NOTICES WHEN A GROUP MEMBERSHIP WAS NOT ADDED.

           Making an account puts the new user into several Windows groups -
           sdusers, sdsshonly, and sdssh or sdapi depending on the access it
           was given.  Each of those was reported as having worked without
           anything checking whether it had.

           The check that was there looked at the wrong thing.  It read the
           last operating-system error, which had been a sound test when this
           code ran an external command, and stopped meaning anything when
           that was replaced with a direct call.  It could sit at zero - "no
           error" - through any number of failed additions.

           The additions themselves were never broken, and nothing about a
           successful CREATE.ACCOUNT changes.  What changes is that a failure
           is now reported instead of being announced as a success, with the
           status code the call actually returned.

31 Aug 26  WEOFSEQ AND OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE NO LONGER REPORT SUCCESS WHEN
           THEY FAILED TO SHORTEN THE FILE.

           Both of these do their work by truncating a file, and neither
           looked at whether the truncate had worked.

           WEOFSEQ put 0 - success - on the stack whatever happened, so a
           program had no way to find out.  What this costs is a stale tail:
           the old content past the new end survives, so a file written by a
           later and shorter run can still carry rows from an earlier, longer
           one.  A file produced that way is the answer somebody takes away,
           which is why this was worth fixing even though nobody has reported
           it.

           OPENSEQ ... OVERWRITE promises the previous content is gone.  If
           the truncate failed the old file stayed as it was, and later writes
           replaced only the front of it.

           Both now report the failure the way the rest of these statements
           already do: STATUS() gives the I/O error, an ON ERROR clause runs,
           and a statement written without one stops rather than carrying on.
           Nothing that used to work behaves differently - the change is only
           visible when the truncate actually fails, which needs a read-only
           file, a mandatory lock or a full disk.

31 Aug 26  DELETE.FILE NOW SAYS SO WHEN IT CANNOT REMOVE A RELOCATED INDEX.

           If a file's alternate key indexes had been moved into a directory
           of their own, DELETE.FILE removed the data and dictionary portions
           and reported each of them, then tried to remove the index directory
           without looking at the result.  A directory it failed to remove was
           left on disk in silence.

           It now prints "Failed to delete index directory" and sets a
           non-zero status, which is what it already did for a data or
           dictionary portion it could not remove.  The file itself was, and
           still is, deleted correctly - what was missing was the report.

31 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR IS NO LONGER SHUT OUT OF ACCOUNTS BY LOGTO.

           Administrators may enter any account without being granted access
           to it, and that had stopped working in two ways.  Signed in as
           yourself, the very first LOGTO was refused with "User not allowed
           in requested account" - which is every session reached over ssh,
           because ssh sessions are never elevated.  Starting elevated, the
           first LOGTO worked and every one after it was refused, including
           going back to SDSYS.

           The cause was that LOGTO asked whether the SESSION still held
           administrative privilege, and leaving SDSYS deliberately gives that
           privilege up.  Entering an account and being allowed to run the
           restricted commands are separate questions, and giving up the
           second no longer takes the first away.

           If you had been reconnecting to get back in, you should not need to
           any more.

31 Aug 26  BEING A WINDOWS ADMINISTRATOR IS NO LONGER ENOUGH ON ITS OWN TO
           REACH SDSYS.

           SD administration needs BOTH: membership of the Windows
           Administrators group, and an SD account created with the
           ADMINISTRATOR tier.  Until now only the Windows half was checked
           when deciding where a session lands, so somebody added to
           Administrators outside SD - by a domain group or a policy - arrived
           in SDSYS even though SD had never granted them anything, and could
           then enter any account.

           They now land in their own account like anybody else, keeping
           everything that account allows.  What they lose is SDSYS and the
           restricted administrator commands.

           NOTHING CHANGES FOR ACCOUNTS SD CREATED.  CREATE.ACCOUNT ...
           ADMINISTRATOR is what adds someone to the Administrators group in
           the first place, and it writes the tier at the same time, so both
           halves have always agreed for accounts made through SD.  The
           account the installer adopts is an ADMINISTRATOR account too.

           If an administrator finds themselves in their own account instead
           of SDSYS after this, their SD account is not an ADMINISTRATOR one:
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT sets the tier.

31 Aug 26  ERROR MESSAGES ARE NO LONGER CUT OFF AFTER THE FIRST LINE.

           Anything SD reported as a program error was truncated at about 84
           characters, so a message written as three lines lost the last two -
           usually the part saying what to do about it.  The space had always
           been there; the limit was simply set wrong.  Long messages now
           print in full.

31 Aug 26  A WRITE THAT NEEDED A LOCK NO LONGER BLAMES THE DISK.

           Writing a record without holding its lock - which a WRITE inside a
           transaction always needs, and so does any system set to MUSTLOCK=1
           - reported "Error 3023 (o/s 0) writing record (Possible full
           disk?)".  The disk was never involved and nothing had been written.

           It now says that no lock is held, that the stored record is
           unchanged, and that READU takes the lock (READVU for a single
           field).  The old wording is still used for real write failures,
           where a full disk genuinely is one of the causes.

31 Aug 26  "sd -cleanup" NOW RELEASES A DEAD SESSION'S TASK LOCKS.

           It already gave back the file, record and group locks a lost
           session was holding, but never its task locks - so a LOCK taken by
           a program that was then killed stayed taken, and no recovery an
           administrator had would shift it.  A nightly job guarded by LOCK 3
           would not run again until SD was restarted.

           If you have been restarting SD to clear a stuck task lock, you
           should not need to any more.

30 Aug 26  PRESSING ENTER AT A Y/N QUESTION NOW ANSWERS IT, AND THE PROMPT
           SAYS WHICH ANSWER YOU GET.

           Prompts are written (y/<n>) or (<y>/n) - the answer in angle
           brackets is what Enter gives you. Before this, several of them
           simply asked again until you typed a letter.

           The default is the cautious answer everywhere it could be: N at
           every question that deletes, overwrites, or replaces something. In
           the editor that covers copying and moving blocks, deleting a block,
           deleting the whole record, and overwriting an existing one.

           Two questions with more than two answers work the same way. Enter
           declines; Q to quit and A for "all" still have to be typed.

30 Aug 26  F1 AT THE COMMAND PROMPT NO LONGER DOES NOTHING.

           It now tells you that SD has no built-in help system, and points at
           the two things on this machine that can answer questions instead:
           "list voc" for the commands this account has, and the Start Menu
           entry SD -> Check the SD installation.

30 Aug 26  A STANDARD ACCOUNT GETS sort.item.

           It has always had list.item, and the two are the same program - one
           sorts and one does not - so withholding only one of them achieved
           nothing. A standard account now has 355 commands rather than 354.
           Programmer and administrator accounts are unchanged.

30 Aug 26  THE SD API CAN AGAIN BE PROVIDED WITHOUT OPENING IT TO THE NETWORK.

           The installer's API question is now two boxes. The first provides
           the API on port 4243; the second, underneath it, lets other
           computers reach it. Tick only the first and the port is open to this
           computer alone, which is what a program running on this machine
           needs.

           Leaving both unticked still means SD opens no API socket at all.
           remote.api on | local | off changes any of this afterwards.

30 Aug 26  A LOGTO THAT IS REFUSED THE DIRECTORY NOW EXPLAINS ITSELF.

           "Unable to change to new directory" is still printed, and is now
           followed by the reason it is nearly always true: Windows fixes your
           group membership when you sign in, so a group create.account added
           afterwards is not in your current sign-in yet. Sign out and back in.

           If that is not it, the message says so and names the directory SD
           tried, rather than insisting on a cause it cannot check.

30 Aug 26  delete.account NOW TAKES AWAY OPERATING-SYSTEM ACCESS WHENEVER IT
           TAKES AWAY THE WINDOWS LOGIN.

           An administrator-tier account is given permission to reach the
           operating system, recorded against the person's Windows login name.
           delete.account removed that permission only when it had also managed
           to remove the account's Windows profile - and a profile cannot be
           removed while its registry hive is still loaded, which is the usual
           state for someone who has recently signed in. So on the ordinary
           path the permission outlived the login it named.

           That mattered because the permission is keyed by NAME. Windows will
           happily issue the same name to a different person later, and they
           would have inherited it.

           It is also taken away now when delete.account finds the Windows
           login already gone - the half-removed account, where the SD side
           outlived the Windows side. Nothing else about the verb changes, and
           an account whose Windows login SD did not create is untouched as
           before.

30 Aug 26  THREE NEW ADMINISTRATOR COMMANDS: remote.api, remote.ssh AND
           ssh.server.

           Until now, whether this machine offered the SD API or accepted ssh
           from other computers could only be decided while installing. These
           change it afterwards, from inside SD. They are administrator
           commands and are refused to anyone else.

               remote.api on | local | off
               remote.ssh on | off
               ssh.server install | remove

           Each reports the current setting if you give it no keyword, and
           changes nothing when it does.

           REMOTE.API HAS THREE SETTINGS, not two. ON means SD listens on port
           4243 and other computers may connect. LOCAL means SD listens and
           only this computer may connect - which is what an application on
           this machine needs. OFF means SD opens no API socket at all.

           ON and OFF change whether SD opens a socket, and SD decides that
           when it starts, so they offer to restart SD. THE RESTART ENDS EVERY
           SD SESSION INCLUDING YOURS, and the question says so before you
           answer it. LOCAL and ON differ only in the firewall and take effect
           at once.

           REMOTE.SSH takes effect immediately and disconnects nobody. OFF does
           not stop the ssh server, it limits it to this computer - SD accounts
           sign in at this keyboard by connecting to "localhost", so that has
           to keep working.

           SSH.SERVER INSTALL downloads the server from Windows Update. It can
           take several minutes with nothing on screen and usually needs a
           restart afterwards. SSH.SERVER REMOVE asks first, because accounts
           SD creates sign in over ssh and nothing else, so removing it leaves
           them no way in at all - and the removal only completes when Windows
           next restarts, so ssh going on working until then is expected.

           None of these touches the ssh client. ssh, scp and sftp are a
           separate part of Windows and are left alone.

30 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER ASKS ABOUT ssh PROPERLY, AND THE STAND-ALONE
           INSTALLATION IS GONE.

           Installing the ssh server and letting other computers reach it are
           two separate questions now, and you are asked both.

           If this machine has no ssh server, you are offered one - and the
           "let other computers connect" box sits underneath it, greyed out
           until you ask for the server. It cannot be ticked on its own,
           because without a server there is nothing to allow.

           If this machine ALREADY has an ssh server, you are asked the second
           question on its own. That question was not asked at all before: the
           installer silently left the firewall exactly as it found it, so a
           machine that already had ssh could not be limited to loopback during
           an install. It can now. The box starts out MATCHING THE FIREWALL
           RULE THIS COMPUTER ALREADY HAS, so if you leave it alone nothing
           changes - tick it to let others in, untick it to limit ssh to this
           machine. SD still does not touch the configuration of an ssh server
           it did not install.

           Declining the ssh server no longer installs one anyway. It used to:
           the ssh box only ever controlled the firewall, so leaving it blank
           still downloaded the OpenSSH capability, started it and rewrote
           sshd_config. An install with no ssh server means nobody can sign in
           to an SD account, including at this keyboard - accounts reach SD by
           "ssh localhost" - so you use SD yourself, as an administrator, by
           typing "sd".

           THE STAND-ALONE INSTALLATION HAS BEEN REMOVED. It offered no ssh,
           no API and a quick install, and it is now what you get by leaving
           both boxes unticked - the same result without a separate choice, and
           without the one decision this installer made that could not be
           undone afterwards. To add ssh or the API later, run the installer
           again and tick the boxes.

           "create.account user" is no longer refused outright on a system with
           no ssh server. It used to be, because such an account could sign in
           nowhere. You already have to say how each account is reached - SSH,
           API, BOTH or NONE - so that is now decided per account, where it is
           actually known, instead of for the whole installation.

           THE API BOX MEANS SOMETHING DIFFERENT. It used to decide who could
           reach a listener that was always started. It now decides whether SD
           opens one at all: unticked, SD installs a configuration with no
           APIPORT and listens on nothing. Note that there is no longer a
           "listening, but only to this machine" setting - if a program on THIS
           computer talks to SD over the API, tick the box.

           If you had a stand-alone installation, it keeps working. The
           $standalone file in SDSYS is now ignored rather than deleted; its
           own text explains what it used to do.

30 Aug 26  SETTING A PASSWORD WORKS FROM SDSYS WHICHEVER WAY YOU REACHED IT.

           There are two ways into SDSYS: start SD from an elevated session, or
           log in to your own account and use LOGTO SDSYS. Only the first could
           set a password. From the second, CREATE.ACCOUNT and MODIFY.PASSWORD
           set the Windows password and then failed with "status 3035" or
           "SD's own credential could not be written", leaving the account
           unfinished - ssh would let it in and the API would refuse it.

           Windows was refusing the write, and it was right to. SD's credential
           store is readable only by the system and by administrators, and
           LOGTO does not change what Windows thinks your session is allowed to
           do, however much authority SD itself has after it. The half of the
           work that changes the Windows password already went through SD's
           elevation helper; the half that writes the credential did not.

           It does now, and the record is read back and checked before SD tells
           you the password is set - so "Password set for account X" means the
           credential is there and can be read, not merely that a write was
           attempted.

30 Aug 26  A FAILED CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER LEAVES A WINDOWS ACCOUNT BEHIND
           WITHOUT SAYING SO.

           When CREATE.ACCOUNT could not set the password and you answered N,
           it said "An account must have a password. Nothing was created." It
           was meant to remove the Windows account it had just made, and it
           tried - but it never looked at whether that worked, and printed the
           same sentence either way.

           From a session that reached SDSYS with LOGTO rather than by starting
           SD elevated, the removal was refused by Windows and the account
           stayed. It was in none of SD's groups, so nothing in SD could see
           it, DELETE.ACCOUNT could not remove it, and the account clean-up an
           uninstall offers could not find it either. It was also not confined
           the way accounts SD finishes creating are, so it could sign in to
           Windows with the password that had been set for it.

           Two changes. The removal now runs the same way every other change
           SD makes to Windows runs, so it works from either kind of session.
           And the result is checked: if the account really cannot be removed,
           SD says so, names it, and says what is true about it - rather than
           telling you nothing was created.

           IF YOU HAVE ONE OF THESE ALREADY, SD cannot help you find it. Look
           for a Windows account with an SD-shaped name that is in none of the
           sd groups, and remove it with Windows' own tools - or run
           CREATE.ACCOUNT for the same name from an elevated session, which
           takes it over and finishes the job.

29 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR REACHES THE OPERATING SYSTEM AGAIN WITHOUT
           ELEVATING.

           An administrator who had not elevated could not use SH, the !
           prefix, OS.EXECUTE, or the full screen editors - EDIT reads the
           same permission. They had to start SD from an elevated terminal to
           get any of it. That was not intended: an administrator is meant to
           have the operating system, ssh and the API by default.

           It happened because the permission record an ADMINISTRATOR account
           used to be given was removed while SD briefly put administrators
           straight into SDSYS at login, where the permission came from being
           elevated instead. That arrangement was withdrawn the same day and
           the record was not put back. It is back now: creating an
           ADMINISTRATOR account writes it again, with both fields set.

           WORTH KNOWING, BECAUSE IT IS DELIBERATE. The record is keyed on the
           Windows user, not on the SD account, and LOGTO does not change the
           Windows user. So an administrator who logs to another account KEEPS
           the operating system there, even though the account itself may not
           have it. ssh and the API do not behave that way - both are checked
           when the connection is made, so neither follows a LOGTO.

           You can still turn it off for a person by editing or removing their
           OS.USERS record; that is what the file is for, and an existing
           record is never overwritten.

           You will see this on a new installation. Installing does not
           overwrite an existing database, so a system that already has one
           keeps the permissions it already has.

29 Aug 26  LISTF NOW DESCRIBES SDSYS'S OWN FILES INSTEAD OF PRINTING "F".

           In the SDSYS account, LISTF printed a bare F in the Description
           column for ten of the sixteen files it lists - $hold, accounts,
           bp, bp.out, gpl.bp, gpl.bp.out, messages, newvoc, os.users and
           qfile. The other six showed real text.

           F is the file's type code, not a description. It appeared because
           the description is looked up in NEWVOC, the vocabulary a newly
           created account is given, and those ten files belong to SDSYS
           alone - so they are not in it, and with nothing to look up the
           display fell back to showing the type code.

           The ten now carry a description of their own. Nothing else about
           them changes, and no account gains access to anything it did not
           have before.

           Two verb entries, EDIT and MICRO, carried their description where
           the type code goes. Both worked - a type code may be followed by
           comment text - but they were the last two entries inconsistent
           with every other verb, and they are now a plain V like the rest.

           You will see this on a new installation. Installing does not
           overwrite an existing database, so a system that already has one
           keeps the vocabulary it already has.

29 Aug 26  UNINSTALLING NOW OFFERS TO REMOVE THE WINDOWS ACCOUNTS SD CREATED,
           AND SAYS SO EITHER WAY.

           Uninstalling removes SD's ssh configuration - the AllowGroups that
           said who could connect and the ForceCommand that put an ssh session
           into SD rather than at a shell prompt. It did not remove the
           Windows accounts SD had created. So those accounts stayed, with
           their passwords, and became ordinary accounts with a shell on the
           machine: the confinement went and the accounts did not.

           Nothing said so. The closing page listed what uninstalling keeps as
           your database, the ssh server and the sdusers group, and never
           mentioned the accounts.

           Two changes:

             * A second question, after the one about the database and like
               it defaulting to No: remove the Windows accounts SD created,
               with their sdu_ and sdg_ groups and their profiles.

             * The closing page now names the accounts in what uninstalling
               keeps, whichever way you answer.

           YOUR OWN ACCOUNT IS NEVER REMOVED by that prompt, and the question
           names it so you can see which one is being kept before you answer.
           The sweep also refuses outright if it would remove the last account
           able to sign in to Windows as an administrator - that refusal
           overrides a Yes.

           It records what it removed and what it kept in a log file, and
           names it when it finishes. A profile whose registry settings are
           still loaded cannot be deleted until the next restart; the log
           names any that were left behind.

29 Aug 26  A COMMIT INSIDE ANOTHER TRANSACTION NO LONGER THROWS AWAY THE
           OUTER TRANSACTION'S WRITES.

           If you began a transaction, wrote a record, began a second
           transaction inside it, and committed that inner one, the outer
           transaction was quietly abandoned at that point. Its writes were
           then lost when you committed it - no error, no warning, nothing in
           the log. The inner records landed and the outer ones did not.

           That is the one thing a transaction exists to prevent, so if you
           have avoided nesting transactions because results looked
           inconsistent, this is why.

           A commit now ends only its own transaction and puts you back in
           the one that contained it, which is what a rollback has always
           done.

           system(1008), the transaction nesting level, is also correct
           again. It only ever counted upwards before - it was raised when a
           transaction began and never lowered when one was committed - so
           after your first commit it could not be used to ask whether you
           were inside a transaction. system(1007) was unaffected and stays
           the reliable test on older releases.

29 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR NOW SIGNS IN TO THEIR OWN ACCOUNT, AND REACHES
           SDSYS BY ELEVATING - NOT AUTOMATICALLY.

           There are two ways into SDSYS and both are deliberate acts.  Start
           SD from a terminal you have already elevated and you land in SDSYS.
           Otherwise you land in your own account, exactly like anybody else,
           and type "logto sdsys" when you want it - which asks for consent at
           that point.

           What changed is the second case.  Being an administrator was enough
           on its own for a moment: any administrator starting SD was sent to
           SDSYS and asked to consent, whether they meant to go there or not.
           Now it is the session that decides, not who you are, so ordinary
           work happens in your own account and administrator rights are
           something you ask for.

           Two consequences worth knowing:

             * Administrators can use ssh again.  An ssh session cannot be
               elevated - the consent dialog has nowhere to appear - so it
               lands in your own account and uses that account's rights.

             * A Windows administrator with no SD account of their own is now
               refused, with "this user is not registered for SD use".  That
               already applied to everybody else; it applies to
               administrators too.  Create an account for them with
               create.account, as you would for anyone.

           Be clear about what the last one is worth: a Windows administrator
           can put themselves back in the group, read the database directly,
           or run as the system account.  It is an explicit step and a record
           of who took it, not a wall.

29 Aug 26  CHANGING AN ACCOUNT'S TIER NOW TELLS YOU WHOSE ACCESS IT BROKE.

           Raising an account's tier can strand a grant.  If b is a standard
           account that alice has been given access to, "modify.account b
           programmer" leaves alice a member of the account's Windows group -
           so nothing looks wrong - while logto refuses her, because a grant
           may go down or sideways and never up.

           modify.account now lists the people whose access it has just made
           useless, and says what to do about it: raise their tier, or revoke
           the grant so the membership does not outlive the access.

           It reports only what that command changed.  A membership that was
           already refused before you ran it - one made outside SD, or left by
           an earlier promotion - is not listed, because it is not this
           command's doing.  Lowering a tier strands nobody and says nothing.

           If the account's group cannot be read, it says so.  Silence means
           "nobody was affected", so the one case where nothing could be
           checked is spelled out rather than looking like good news.

29 Aug 26  ACCESS TO AN ACCOUNT CAN NO LONGER BE GRANTED UPWARDS, AND SDSYS
           CANNOT BE GRANTED AT ALL.

           Accounts have a tier - standard, programmer or administrator - and
           a grant may now go down or sideways, never up.  A programmer may be
           given access to a standard account; a standard user may not be
           given access to a programmer account.  Where that was allowed
           before, standing in the other account gave you its whole verb set,
           so a grant could hand somebody more than their own tier permits.

           Entry to SDSYS is never granted to anybody, whatever their tier.
           It belongs to Windows administrators, who reach it by signing in or
           with LOGTO.

           The rule is applied in three places and they are not equivalent.
           GRANT and MODIFY.ACCOUNT ... ADD refuse at the time you ask, so you
           find out immediately.  LOGTO refuses at the door, and that is the
           one that actually holds - a grant is membership of a Windows group,
           so it can be made outside SD, and an account's tier can be raised
           after a grant was legitimately given.  An existing membership that
           the rule no longer permits therefore stops working at LOGTO rather
           than being quietly honoured.

           REVOKE and MODIFY.ACCOUNT ... DELETE are unaffected.  Taking access
           away is always allowed.

28 Aug 26  A DELETED ACCOUNT'S WINDOWS PROFILE IS NOW CLEARED UP BY ITSELF,
           AND CREATE.ACCOUNT REFUSES A NAME WHOSE OLD PROFILE IS STILL THERE.

           Windows cannot delete a user profile while it still has that
           profile's registry settings loaded, which is the ordinary state
           after somebody has signed in - so DELETE.ACCOUNT could remove the
           account and not its home directory under C:\Users.  Only a restart
           releases it.

           What that cost was invisible and turned up much later.  Windows
           will not put a new profile where one already sits: an account
           created again under the same name was given a DIFFERENT home
           directory - the name with your computer's name added - and
           anything in the old one, ssh keys included, was not found there.
           The symptom people met was ssh refusing a key that had been
           working, days after the deletion, with nothing connecting the two.

           Two changes:

             * DELETE.ACCOUNT now keeps both halves of a profile it cannot
               remove and records it.  THE NEXT TIME THE COMPUTER STARTS, the
               SD service clears the directory and the registry entry
               together, without being asked.  Nothing has to be done by
               hand, and no restart is asked for at the time - it rides the
               next one that happens anyway.

             * CREATE.ACCOUNT refuses to create an account whose profile
               directory is still sitting there, and names the directory,
               rather than quietly giving the account the wrong home.  On a
               machine where SD deleted the old account, restarting and
               trying again is the whole of the answer.

           DELETE.ACCOUNT says which of the three states it reached, and one
           of them still needs a person: if SD could not record the profile
           for clearing, it says so and tells you what to remove.

           There is also a new folder, C:\ProgramData\SD\profile-reclaim.  It
           holds those records and nothing else, it is readable only by
           administrators, and it is normally empty.

28 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT NOW SETS THE PASSWORD FOR THE API AS WELL AS ssh.

           The password you are asked for when an account is created is used
           for both ways in.  Before, it was set only as the Windows password,
           which is what ssh checks; SD keeps its own credential for the API,
           and nothing wrote one until MODIFY.PASSWORD was run.  So a new
           account created with BOTH was told

               SD routes for <name>: ssh and the API.

           and the API then refused it, with a message that could not say why:
           the same refusal covers "no such account" and "you were not granted
           this account", deliberately, so the API does not tell an outsider
           which accounts exist.

           If you have an account that the API refuses and ssh admits, run

               modify.password <name>

           as an administrator and it will work.  Accounts created from now on
           need nothing.

           The two are still separate credentials, and MODIFY.PASSWORD still
           changes SD's without touching the Windows one.  Creation simply
           gives them the same value, which is what typing "the password for
           this account" is meant to mean.

28 Aug 26  ENCRYPT.FIELD IS NO LONGER IN AN ADMINISTRATOR'S VOC.

           The verb was in every administrator account and could not work:
           it points at a program called $CRYPTO which is not part of this
           distribution, so typing it answered

               Unable to load '$CRYPTO' object code at line 1550 of $CPROC

           which names neither the verb you typed nor anything you could act
           on.  "Verb not found" is the honest answer, and that is what you get
           now.  Nothing in the documentation referred to the verb, and field
           encryption was not available through it before.

           An ADMINISTRATOR account's VOC therefore holds 416 records rather
           than 417.  STANDARD (354) and PROGRAMMER (396) are unchanged.

28 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT SAYS WHY A PASSWORD WAS NOT SET.

           It warned that the account was created but the password was not,
           offered a retry, and gave no reason.  The two likeliest causes need
           opposite responses: if the two entries simply did not match, typing
           them again fixes it, but if WINDOWS refused the password for not
           meeting the machine's policy, retyping the same one will fail for
           ever.  Each case now says which it was, and a session that is not
           running elevated is told that retrying cannot help.

28 Aug 26  SEVEN FIXES TO EVERYDAY COMMANDS.

           NO.QUERY IS NOW HONOURED WHEN A FILE LIVES IN THE SYSTEM ACCOUNT.
           "delete.file <name> no.query" could still stop and ask whether to
           delete the file from the system account.  Typed at a terminal that
           was merely surprising; driven from a script it was worse, because
           the question read the script's next line as its answer.  NO.QUERY
           now takes the safe answer - the VOC reference goes, the file in the
           system account stays - and says that is what it did.

           DELETE.FILE NO LONGER ASKS TWICE ABOUT A NAME TYPED IN LOWER CASE.
           The file name is stored upper case and was compared against the name
           you typed, so "delete.file zzwork" asked "OK to delete DATA portion"
           and "OK to delete DICT portion" about a file that was exactly where
           it should be.  The comparison now ignores case.

           DELETE.INDEX ACCEPTS AN INDEX NAME IN LOWER CASE.  "list.index zzak
           f1" found the index and "delete.index zzak f1" answered
           "Unrecognised index name (f1)".  Both now match the way LIST.INDEX
           always did.  A name that really is unknown is still reported exactly
           as you typed it.

           ".D name" FINDS A RECORD SAVED IN LOWER CASE.  A paragraph saved as
           "daily" could be listed with ".L DAILY" and recalled with ".R DAILY"
           but not deleted with ".D DAILY".  It also now says "not found in
           VOC" when there is no such record, instead of silently doing
           something based on whatever it had read last.

           QSELECT SAYS WHICH LIST IT FILLED.  Every successful QSELECT ended
           with "records selected to select list " and no number.

           MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD AND DELETE ARE NOW AUDITED.  Adding or removing
           somebody's access to an account with MODIFY.ACCOUNT wrote no record
           in SD's audit file, while GRANT and REVOKE - which make the same
           change - always did.  Both routes now leave a trail.

           CREATE.ACCOUNT NO LONGER SEEMS TO CONTRADICT ITSELF.  It printed
           "<name> may sign in over ssh only" and then "<name> may sign in over
           ssh and use the API", which cannot both be true of one thing.  They
           were about two different things: how you reach the COMPUTER, and
           which SD ROUTES the account has.  Each line now names its subject.

27 Aug 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW CLEARS THE WINDOWS PROFILE RECORD TOO.

           If you deleted an account and then created another one with the
           SAME NAME, the new account quietly got a DIFFERENT home directory -
           C:\Users\<name> with a suffix added, rather than C:\Users\<name>.
           Anything kept in the old home was then not where the account looked
           for it, and the clearest symptom was ssh: a key that had worked
           stopped being accepted, with a message that said nothing about why.

           Deleting an account removes the Windows account and its profile.
           Removing the profile has two halves - the folder, and a record
           Windows keeps of where that folder is - and when the folder could
           not be removed, which is normal if anybody had used the account
           recently, NEITHER half was removed.

           EITHER HALF ON ITS OWN IS ENOUGH TO CAUSE THIS.  Windows will not
           put a new profile where a record already points, and it will not put
           one where a folder already sits; in both cases it adds a suffix to
           the name instead.  So both halves are now removed in their own
           right, and recreating an account under an old name gives it the home
           directory you would expect.

           THE FOLDER OFTEN CANNOT BE REMOVED, AND THAT IS WINDOWS RATHER THAN
           SD.  If the account has ever signed in, Windows keeps its registry
           settings mounted afterwards, which locks a file inside the folder;
           while that lasts the folder cannot be deleted or even renamed, by
           anybody, however privileged.  Only restarting the computer releases
           it.

           So DELETE.ACCOUNT says which half was left and what to do about it,
           and neither warning is cosmetic: until that half is cleared, the
           next account of the same name will still be pushed elsewhere.  For
           an account that never signed in there is nothing mounted and both
           halves go cleanly.

27 Aug 26  TWO EDITOR AND TERMINAL FIXES.

           MICRO NO LONGER REPORTS "Permission denied" WHEN YOU SAVE.  Every
           save from an ordinary account showed

               Permission denied. Save with sudo not supported on Windows

           in red at the bottom of the screen.  YOUR WORK WAS ACTUALLY SAVED
           EVERY TIME - the message was wrong, not the save - but there was no
           way to know that from the screen.

           The cause was where micro kept its settings.  It was told to use a
           directory under Program Files, which ordinary accounts can read and
           not write, and micro writes there whenever it saves.  It now uses
           one in your own home directory instead:

               C:\Users\<you>\.micro

           created for you the first time you run "micro", with SD's BASIC
           syntax colouring copied into it.  Nothing to set up, and your micro
           settings now persist between sessions.  If your account has no
           writable home directory, SD uses a temporary one instead, and the
           editor still works.

           An elevated session was never affected, which is why this went
           unnoticed at first.  The EDIT verb - Microsoft Edit - never had the
           problem at all.

           TERM DEFAULT NOW RESTORES 120 x 36, not 20 x 24.  "term default"
           was setting the page to the smallest size SD allows, which wrapped
           every standard report.  It now sets the documented default width and
           depth, the same size a session starts with when nothing else tells
           it otherwise.

27 Aug 26  AN ACCOUNT'S TIER CAN BE CHANGED AFTER IT IS CREATED, AND THERE IS
           A FOURTH ONE: SUSPENDED.

           Until now the tier was fixed when the account was made.
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT can now move it in either direction:

               modify.account don programmer both
               modify.account ann administrator
               modify.account bob suspended
               modify.account bob standard

           The keyword says what the account IS, the same way SSH, API, BOTH
           and NONE do, and the first three words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's.

           SUSPENDED DENIES ACCESS WITHOUT CHANGING ANYTHING ELSE.  It is for
           an account that should stop working for a while - somebody on
           leave, a login being investigated - and it is refused at all three
           ways in:

               ssh or the console      Account BOB is suspended
               logto from another      Account BOB is suspended
               the API                 User not allowed in requested account

           Nothing is taken away on Windows and the account's VOC is left
           exactly as it is, so lifting a suspension puts the account back as
           it was with nothing to remember.  The tier it displaced is kept, and
           LIST ACCOUNTS shows both.

           THERE IS NO "RESUME" KEYWORD.  Coming back always names the tier the
           account is to have:

               modify.account bob programmer

           and no intermediate step is ever needed - an account can go
           programmer, standard, suspended and back to any of them directly.

           THE VOC CHANGES AT ONCE, not at the next login.  A downgrade removes
           the verbs the lower tier does not get and an upgrade copies them in,
           and the verb says how many of each:

               Account ANN is now STANDARD
               VOC: 0 records added, 41 removed, 1 left alone

           "LEFT ALONE" IS THE ONE TO READ.  A record is only removed if it is
           still exactly what SD put there.  Anything edited or replaced in the
           account is counted and kept, because it is somebody's work under a
           verb's name rather than the verb.

           LEAVING ADMINISTRATOR TAKES AWAY MORE THAN THE VERBS, and one part
           has to be spelt out:

               the Windows Administrators group    removed
               the OS.USERS record                 removed
               ssh and the API                     you say

           The first two were the account's BECAUSE it was an administrator.
           ssh and the API are a rule for an administrator and a setting for
           everybody else, so the command has to say which:

               :modify.account don programmer
               Say what remote access DON is to have: ssh, api, both or none
               :modify.account don programmer both
               DON may sign in over ssh and use the API
               Account DON is now PROGRAMMER

           WHAT IS REFUSED:

             - Suspending your own account, or the one you are standing in.
             - Making a group account an ADMINISTRATOR; there is no single
               person behind it to put in a Windows group.
             - SDSYS, which MODIFY.ACCOUNT has never accepted.

           AN ELEVATED SESSION CAN STILL "LOGTO" A SUSPENDED ACCOUNT.  That is
           deliberate: looking at a suspended account is the usual reason to
           have one, and anyone elevated could lift the suspension anyway.  A
           suspension denies the account's own user.

           LIST ACCOUNTS NOW SHOWS THE TIER, and this changes its default
           columns - the tier replaces the Windows group, which was always
           "sdu_" or "sdg_" followed by the account name and told you nothing
           the id did not.  Both are still there by name:

               list accounts
               list accounts path descr group tier
               list accounts tier prior.tier

27 Aug 26  AN ADMINISTRATOR NO LONGER HAS TO ELEVATE TO REACH THE OPERATING
           SYSTEM.

           An SD account made with the ADMINISTRATOR tier is now added to the
           SD system file OS.USERS as it is created, with both fields set to
           "yes".  That is the list SD reads to decide who may use the shell
           and who may run a program outside SD, so from an ordinary,
           unelevated session an administrator can now use:

               sh  and  !                the shell
               OS.EXECUTE                from a BASIC program
               edit  and  micro          the full-screen editors

           ssh and the API already worked this way - an administrator has had
           both since 21 Aug and no keyword can take either away.  OS.EXECUTE
           was the one route still asking for elevation, and on a fresh
           install OS.USERS was empty, so the person who installed SD was
           refused the editors in their own account.

           IT APPLIES TO THE ACCOUNT THE INSTALLER MAKES FOR YOU, which is an
           ADMINISTRATOR account, so this is the ordinary case rather than a
           special one.

           FOR EVERY OTHER TIER IT IS A GRANT, and there are now keywords
           for it:

               create.account user ann programmer sh-on
               create.account user bob programmer os-on
               modify.account ann os-on
               modify.account ann sh-off

           sh-on and os-on on CREATE.ACCOUNT; sh-on, sh-off, os-on and os-off
           on MODIFY.ACCOUNT.  They are four switches over two fields rather
           than four names for one state, so sh-off leaves OS.EXECUTE alone.
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT needs an elevated session, as it always has - you
           elevate to grant somebody the right not to have to.

           FOR AN ADMINISTRATOR ALL THREE ROUTES ARE A RULE, and MODIFY.ACCOUNT
           refuses to change any of them:

               :modify.account don os-off
               don is an administrator and always reaches the operating system

           ssh and the API have refused this since 21 Aug; the operating
           system now refuses it too, in both directions.  The whole purpose of
           the ADMINISTRATOR tier is that it grants unlimited access.

           The record itself is ordinary data and can also be edited by hand:

               logto sdsys
               ed os.users don

           Field 1 is SH and field 2 is OS.EXECUTE, each "yes" or anything
           else.  The name is the WINDOWS login name, not the SD account name,
           because the permission belongs to the person and does not change
           when they LOGTO somewhere else.

           AN ACCOUNT OF ANY OTHER TIER IS UNCHANGED: no record, and no
           operating system, until an administrator adds one.  An elevated
           session still passes whatever the file says, so an empty OS.USERS
           cannot lock the machine's own administrator out.

           AND DELETE.ACCOUNT NOW TAKES THE RECORD AWAY AGAIN, but only when
           it is deleting the Windows account itself.  Deleting an SD account
           whose Windows login stays behind leaves the person's permission
           alone, which is the right way round for a list keyed by person.

26 Aug 26  SYSTEM(1010) SAID "Linux".  IT NOW SAYS "Windows".

           A BASIC program asking SD what platform it was running on was told
           Linux, on Windows.  The name was inherited from the Linux original,
           where it is correct, and never changed - the same oversight as
           SYSTEM(91), which was fixed on 17 Aug.

           IT ALSO CHANGES A COMPILER TOKEN.  The BASIC compiler defines a
           token from this name for use with $IFDEF, so what used to be
           SD.LINUX is now SD.WINDOWS.  Nothing shipped with SD tests either
           name, so no supplied program compiles differently - but if you have
           written "$IFDEF SD.LINUX" against this port, it is now SD.WINDOWS,
           which is the name you would have expected in the first place.

           SYSTEM(91) - "is this Windows" - has answered 1 since 17 Aug and is
           the one to use for a straight platform test.

26 Aug 26  CONFIG() WITH A LONG NAME NO LONGER STOPS YOUR PROGRAM.

           Configuration parameter names are at most eight characters.  Asking
           for a longer one - CONFIG("NOSUCHKEY") is nine - used to abort the
           program with "Data cannot be converted to a string", which named
           nothing to do with the real problem.

           It now does what an unknown eight-character name has always done:
           returns an empty string and sets STATUS() to 1004, item not found.
           A name that is too long is a name that does not exist, and you can
           now test for it instead of guarding against it.

           Names are also CASE SENSITIVE and upper case - CONFIG("NUMLOCKS")
           answers, CONFIG("numlocks") does not.  That has not changed.

26 Aug 26  SET.SOCKET.MODE CAN NOW TURN KEEP-ALIVE OFF.

           SET.SOCKET.MODE(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE, 0) returned 1, for
           success, and switched keep-alive ON.  The value you passed was read
           and then discarded.  SOCKET.INFO(skt, SKT$INFO.KEEP.ALIVE) was
           telling the truth all along, so the two disagreed.

           The setting is now whatever you asked for.  If you have code that
           worked around this by not calling it, the workaround is no longer
           needed.

26 Aug 26  CLEAR SCREEN AND CURSOR POSITIONING NOW WORK IN A POWERSHELL
           WINDOW.

           They already worked from cmd and in Windows Terminal.  Started from
           PowerShell, "@(-1)" and "@(x,y)" did nothing at all - output ran
           down the screen as though the terminal had no cursor control.

           WHY, AND IT WAS NEVER ABOUT POWERSHELL ITSELF.  The default terminal
           type is "windows", a clone of xterm, so SD draws with ANSI escape
           sequences.  A Windows console only acts on those when the flag
           ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING is set on it, and that flag
           belongs to the WINDOW rather than to the program running in it.
           cmd.exe sets it; Windows Terminal is built around it; Windows
           PowerShell 5.1 does not set it.  SD had no opinion of its own - it
           used whatever its launcher happened to leave behind.

           SD NOW TURNS IT ON FOR ITSELF at start-up, and puts it back the way
           it found it on the way out.  Nothing to configure, and no difference
           between the three windows any more.

           This does not apply to a session over ssh, which was never affected
           - your ssh client interprets the escapes at your end.

26 Aug 26  MISTYPING THE PASSWORD AT THE END OF AN INSTALL NO LONGER LEAVES
           THE ACCOUNT WITHOUT ONE.

           The installer finishes by opening SD so you can set a password.
           Typing the two entries differently used to end it there - "Passwords
           do not match.  Password not changed." - and the install carried on
           to its check with no password set.  ONE MISTYPE, ONE CHANCE.

           You now get three, and it tells you which attempt you are on.

           AND IT NOW TELLS YOU WHAT ENTER DOES BEFORE YOU PRESS IT.  The
           prompt is preceded by:

               Press Enter on an empty line to set no password for now.
               The account then works only at this computer, from a
               session run as administrator; ssh and the SD API will
               refuse to connect until a password is set.  SD asks
               again the first time you open the account.

           Changing a password you already have says the other thing that
           is true there instead: Enter leaves it unchanged.

           THAT IS THE FIRST LINE ONLY, and the prompts look alike, so it is
           worth saying: pressing Enter at "Repeat new password" is not
           declining.  It simply fails to match and costs one of the three
           attempts.

           PRESSING ENTER ON AN EMPTY LINE DECLINES AT ONCE and is not counted
           as an attempt.  Declining is a decision; being re-asked would turn
           it into an argument.

           NOTHING WAS EVER LOST BY IT.  SD asks again the first time you open
           the account, and that prompt is bounded at three now as well.

26 Aug 26  CONFIG GPL AND CONFIG CONTRIB WORK AGAIN.

           Both did nothing on Windows, and for two reasons either of which
           was enough on its own:

           * they ran an external pager, "less", which is not on a Windows
             machine and which SD does not install; and
           * they reached it through "!", the shell escape, so an ordinary
             user was refused before that even mattered - with a message
             about the operating system shell that named neither CONFIG nor
             the licence.

           SHOWING A LICENCE SHOULD NOT REQUIRE SHELL PERMISSION, so neither
           command shells out now.  The text is held inside SD as a record in
           the SDSYS VOC and printed with SD's own pagination, which works at
           the console and over ssh alike and needs nothing installed.

           THE LICENCE FILE STILL SHIPS, in the data tree as before.  That is
           deliberate rather than left over: SD is GPL-3 and that file is the
           only copy of the licence in the distribution, so it has to be
           readable by somebody who has not started SD.

           AND THE LICENCE TEXT NAMED A LICENCE SD DOES NOT USE.  It said the
           install and delete scripts were under the Black Oak Model License
           v1.0.0.  Those were the Linux shell scripts, which this port
           replaced entirely; nothing in SD Core carries that licence and no
           source file ever mentioned it.  The line is gone.

           What is left is correct and unchanged: most of SD is GPL v3.0, the
           API is LGPL v3.0, and anything not marked otherwise is GPL v3.0.

26 Aug 26  THERE ARE FULL-SCREEN EDITORS AGAIN: EDIT AND MICRO.

           Two verbs, two editors, and they work the same way.  Each opens a
           record in a terminal editor, saves it back, and offers to compile
           and catalogue a BP record - the loop MICRO had before it was
           removed on 17 Aug.

               edit  bp myprog          Microsoft Edit
               micro bp myprog          micro
               edit  dict customers name

           WHO HAS THEM, AND IT IS TWO THINGS RATHER THAN ONE.

           * THE VERB: programmer and administrator accounts get both.  A
             standard account gets neither, for the same reason it has no ED -
             it is not a development account.
           * OS.EXECUTE PERMISSION: an editor runs outside SD, so reaching one
             is reaching the operating system.  That is field 2 of your record
             in the SD system file os.users - the same field that governs
             OS.EXECUTE from inside a program - and only an administrator can
             change it.

           An elevated session passes on its own, as it does for SH, so an
           empty list cannot lock the machine's own administrator out.  A
           missing record means no.

           IF YOU HAVE THE VERB AND NOT THE PERMISSION you are told so by name
           and told what to ask for, rather than the command appearing to do
           nothing.  A session with no terminal - an API session, or a piped
           script - is refused first and separately: there is nowhere to draw
           a full screen.

           THEY WORK OVER ssh, which is the point of a terminal editor.  But
           an ssh session can never be elevated, so an administrator arriving
           that way needs an os.users entry the same as anybody else.

           ONLY MICRO HIGHLIGHTS SD BASIC.  Microsoft Edit has no syntax
           highlighting, and that is the one real difference between the two
           verbs.  MICRO colours statements, reserved words, intrinsic
           functions, @variables, $directives, labels, strings, numbers and
           comments.

           It applies to a BP record and to nothing else - SD names the
           working copy so micro can recognise the language, and a record
           edited out of any other file is plain text.

           The word lists are GENERATED FROM THE COMPILER's own tables - 218
           statements, 37 reserved words and 176 intrinsic functions - so the
           highlighting cannot drift from the language.  If a name you expect
           is not coloured, that is worth reporting.

           NOTHING IS INSTALLED INTO YOUR PROFILE.  SD ships the rules with
           itself and points micro at them, so they work for every account on
           the machine - including the ones that cannot log in to Windows and
           so have no profile to put them in.

           TO GRANT IT, from an elevated session:

               logto sdsys
               ed os.users don

           Two lines and nothing else: field 1 is SH, field 2 is OS.EX.  "yes"
           in field 2 is what the editors need.  The name is the WINDOWS login
           name, not the SD account name, and the change takes effect on that
           person's next command rather than at their next login.

           WHAT THE EDITORS ARE GOOD FOR: BASIC source, VOC records and simple
           dictionary records - anything whose content is lines of text.  SD
           writes one field per line, so moving between fields is moving
           between lines.

           MULTIVALUES AND SUBVALUES ARE TYPEABLE, AND THIS IS NEW.  Value
           and subvalue marks are control characters an editor cannot show, so
           each has a token you type instead:

               ~~   a value mark
               ~`   a subvalue mark
               ~!   a text mark
               ~-   a literal ~, where one would otherwise be misread
               ~,   a literal , where one would otherwise be a separator

           EVERY TOKEN IS ~ AND ONE MORE CHARACTER, and ~ is the only escape
           character.  SD converts marks to tokens on the way into the editor
           and back again on the way out.  SMITH~~JONES~~BROWN is a
           three-value field; RED~`BLUE~~GREEN is two values, the first with
           two subvalues.

           * MARKS IN A ROW ARE SEPARATED BY A COMMA.  Written token against
             token a run of marks cannot be read, so a text mark, a text mark
             and a value mark, one after another, is:

                 ~!,~!,~~

             Type the comma yourself when you enter marks in a row.  It is a
             separator and not data, which is why a literal comma standing in
             exactly that position - between two marks - is written ~,.
           * THE CONVERSION IS LOSSLESS.  No record is refused and none is
             mangled, whatever it contains.  SD writes a tilde as ~- only
             where the character after it would otherwise make the pair look
             like a token - another ~, a backtick, a !, a hyphen, a comma, or
             a mark.  EVERYWHERE ELSE A TILDE IS LEFT ALONE, so ordinary text
             reads exactly as you wrote it and a~b is still a~b.  Type ~-
             yourself when you want a literal ~ in one of those positions.
           * A compiled dictionary record is truncated to its first 15 fields
             while you edit it and recompiled with CD when you save, which is
             what MICRO always did.

           ***GIVE THESE VERBS ONLY TO PEOPLE YOU TRUST.***  An editor can
           write anywhere its user can write.  It opens the record you named,
           but nothing then stops the person opening any other file on the
           machine their Windows account may open, inside the SD data tree or
           outside it.  That is not a hole in SD - it is what an editor is -
           and it is why these verbs are behind OS.EXECUTE permission and not
           merely behind the programmer tier.

           ED IS UNCHANGED and is still there.  If you preferred the line
           editor, nothing has been taken away.  What HAS changed is that
           "EDIT" used to be a second name for ED, so if you have been typing
           EDIT to get the line editor you will now get a full screen.

           THE EDITOR IS FOUND, NOT ASSUMED.  Current Windows builds carry
           edit.exe already; micro never ships with Windows.  The installer
           installs whichever is missing, with winget, and if that cannot be
           done - no winget, no network, a policy in the way - the install
           still succeeds and says so in
           C:\ProgramData\SD\install-editors.log.  The verb then refuses
           with the command that installs it rather than appearing to do
           nothing.  To do it by hand, from an elevated prompt:

               winget install -e --id Microsoft.Edit  --scope machine
               winget install -e --id zyedidia.micro  --scope machine

           WHY THE SCOPE MATTERS: without it winget installs into the profile
           of whoever ran it, and accounts SD creates cannot log in to Windows
           at all, so a per-user copy is one they can never reach.

           WHAT IT MEANS FOR CONTAINMENT, because 17 Aug removed MICRO on
           exactly this ground.  An editor can open any file the person
           running it is allowed to open, so these verbs widen what a
           programmer can reach beyond SD's own files.  Neither is a shell -
           neither editor can run a command - and neither is available to
           standard accounts or over the API.  The line SD draws is the
           account tier, not the OS.USERS list that governs SH.

26 Aug 26  THE STAND-ALONE INSTALLER NO LONGER OFFERS TO OPEN THE API PORT.

           A stand-alone install opens no network port at all - it writes an
           sd.conf with no APIPORT line, so SD never creates the socket.  The
           installer's "Remote access" page was nevertheless still showing the
           option "Let other computers on your network connect to the SD API
           (port 4243)".

           Ticking it did nothing, and nothing was ever left exposed by it:
           the firewall step already checked the install mode and stopped.  But
           the option appeared two screens after the mode page had said that no
           port would be opened, which is a contradiction whether or not it has
           any effect.  It is now hidden on a stand-alone install, the same way
           the matching ssh option already was.

           A full installation is unchanged and still offers both.

25 Aug 26  VIRTUAL FILE SYSTEM SUPPORT IS GONE FROM THE LANGUAGE TOO.

           SD has never been able to open a virtual file system.  Nothing in
           the file-opening code has ever recognised a "VFS:" pathname.  What
           the language carried was the outline of one - a file type FTYPE
           would report, three error numbers, and a set of names in the SYSCOM
           include files - and none of it could be reached.  A VOC F-pointer
           written as "VFS:something" was reported as a virtual file system,
           passed the name resolver, and then failed to open with an unrelated
           error.

           ALL OF IT HAS BEEN REMOVED, so the language no longer offers a
           feature it cannot perform.

           WHAT THIS CHANGES FOR YOU.  These names are no longer defined, and
           a program that mentions one will no longer compile:

              FL$TYPE.VFS            SYSCOM KEYS.H
              ER$VFS.NAME            SYSCOM ERR.H
              ER$VFS.CLASS           SYSCOM ERR.H
              ER$VFS.NGLBL           SYSCOM ERR.H

           FTYPE no longer returns "VFS" for a "VFS:" pathname - it treats it
           as an ordinary pathname, which will not open either.  Error numbers
           3038, 3039 and 3040 are retired and will not be given a new meaning.

           IF ONE OF YOUR PROGRAMS REFERS TO ANY OF THESE, it was testing for
           a state SD could not reach, and the test can be deleted.

           TWO MORE UNREACHABLE PIECES WENT WITH IT.  SD used to install a
           program called _EXTENDLIST into the SDSYS GPL.BP file and load it
           at every start-up; it existed only to serve a virtual file system,
           and nothing has ever called it.  It is gone.  And the debugger no
           longer offers "(Networked)" as a file type - remote files over
           SDNet were removed earlier in this port, so no file could report
           it.

25 Aug 26  THE FIVE PROGRAMS SD SHIPPED INTO THE SDSYS BP FILE ARE GONE.

           SD used to install PCL and PCL.GRID (printer control), U0032 and
           U50BB (user exits) and VFS.CLS (a template class module) into the
           SDSYS account's BP file.  None of them is needed in this version,
           and SD does not support virtual file systems, so the template was
           describing something you cannot use.  PCL is unaffected as a
           printer feature - the PCL keyword and the catalogued PCL routine
           are both still there; what has gone is a second, older copy of the
           source sitting in BP.

           SD NOW SHIPS NOTHING INTO THE SDSYS BP FILE.  It is created empty
           and is yours to use.

           AND BECAUSE OF THAT, BP AND ITS COMPILED OBJECTS ARE NOW PRESERVED
           WHEN YOU UPGRADE, alongside your accounts, the private catalogue
           and the rest of your own data.  Anything you have written into the
           SDSYS BP file, and anything you have compiled from it, survives an
           upgrade untouched.

25 Aug 26  LETTING OTHER COMPUTERS REACH THE SD API IS NOW OFF UNTIL YOU
           ASK FOR IT.

           The installer offers two remote-access options: one for ssh and one
           for the SD API.  The ssh one has always been off unless you tick
           it.  The API one used to be ON unless you cleared it, so an
           installation left at its defaults opened port 4243 to your local
           network.

           BOTH ARE NOW OFF UNLESS YOU TICK THEM.  On a default installation
           the SD API can be reached from this computer only.

           NOTHING ELSE ABOUT THE API CHANGED.  It is still switched on, still
           listening on port 4243, and programs running on this computer
           connect to it exactly as before.  What changed is the firewall rule
           the installer leaves behind.

           IF YOU WANT A CLIENT ON ANOTHER COMPUTER, tick "Let other computers
           on your network connect to the SD API" during the install.  If you
           have already installed and need it afterwards, run this from an
           elevated PowerShell prompt:

               powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\api-firewall.ps1" -Open

25 Aug 26  THERE IS NOW A STAND-ALONE INSTALLATION, FOR ONE PERSON AT ONE
           COMPUTER.

           The installer asks which kind you want, on a new page just after
           the welcome page, and describes both in full before you choose.
           You are only asked on a first install: upgrading keeps whatever
           the computer already is.

           CHOOSE THE FULL INSTALLATION if more than one person will use SD,
           or if a program needs to connect to it.  It is what the installer
           has always done and it is still the default.

           CHOOSE THE STAND-ALONE INSTALLATION to learn SD or try some code
           out on your own machine.  It is not meant to be a production
           system.  On it:

             - No ssh server is installed and no ssh configuration is
               changed.  If you already have an ssh server, of any make, SD
               leaves it alone.  scp and sftp GO ON WORKING - on a full
               installation they stop working for everyone.
             - No network port is opened.  The SD API is switched off in
               sd.conf, so SD is not listening on anything and there is no
               firewall rule to open.
             - There is one SD account and it is yours.  You use SD by
               typing  sd  at this computer.
             - create.account user  is refused, and says why.  The Windows
               account it would make is denied the console and Remote
               Desktop because it is meant to arrive over ssh, so with no ssh
               server it could sign in nowhere at all.
             - create.account group  still works, and is how you keep
               separate work separate.  Use  logto <name>  to move into one,
               from a session run as administrator.  No Windows account and
               no sign-in is involved.

           NOTHING ELSE IS CUT DOWN: same SD, same language, same database,
           same commands.  What is missing is the ways in from somewhere
           else.

           TO CHANGE YOUR MIND you have to uninstall and install again.
           Nothing inside SD converts one into the other, and deleting the
           marker file SD leaves in sdsys does not do it either - no ssh
           server would be installed and the API would still be off.

25 Aug 26  INSTALLING A NEW RELEASE OVER AN EXISTING ONE NOW UPDATES YOUR
           DATABASE.  UNTIL NOW IT SILENTLY DID NOT.

           Your database lives in C:\ProgramData\SD, and the installer used to
           leave it completely alone if it was already there.  That protected
           your data, but it also meant a new release's programs and messages
           never reached a computer that already had SD - so a fix could be
           released and you would not get it, with nothing to say so.

           NOW THE INSTALLER REPLACES THE PARTS SD SHIPS AND LEAVES YOUR OWN
           ALONE.  Replaced: the catalogue and compiled programs, the BASIC
           source, the messages, the include records, the VOC templates, the
           library routines, the SDSYS BP programs, terminfo, the licence and
           the contributor list.

           KEPT, AND NOT TOUCHED: your accounts and their passwords, the
           private catalogue, which Windows users are linked to which SD
           accounts, the commands each account may run, your print queue and
           held reports, everything under your own accounts, and sd.conf.
           Anything SD created while it was running - your VOC included - is
           left exactly as it is.

           THE DICTIONARIES ARE BROUGHT UP TO DATE FOR YOU.  Upgrading now
           reapplies the dictionary definitions this release ships, so a field
           added since your last install is recognised.  It ADDS AND UPDATES
           the entries SD ships and leaves alone any you added yourself - if
           you have written your own dictionary items, they survive.  If that
           step cannot run, the installer says so at the end rather than
           finishing quietly, and upgrade-dicts.log in C:\ProgramData\SD says
           what happened.

           ONE THING YOU STILL DO YOURSELF.  Run  update.accounts  in each
           account after upgrading, to bring that account's VOC up to date
           with the new release.  That is what the verb is for, and this
           changelog has been saying so for years.

           If you would rather start clean, uninstalling still offers to
           remove the database and still defaults to keeping it.

25 Aug 26  THE CHANGELOG - THIS FILE - HAS MOVED TO
           C:\Program Files\SD\changelog.

           It used to be installed into the database, which the installer
           never overwrote.  So your copy of it was frozen on the day you
           first installed SD, and could never tell you about anything that
           changed afterwards - in the one file whose entire job is telling
           you what changed.

           In its new home it is replaced every time you install a new
           release, and removed when you uninstall.  Nothing else about it
           changes.  The old frozen copy in the database is removed for you
           when you upgrade, so there is only ever one.

25 Aug 26  SD NOW REFUSES TO INSTALL IF THIS COMPUTER HAS A DIFFERENT ssh
           SERVER, OR IF SOMEBODY HAS ALREADY CONFIGURED THE WINDOWS ONE.

           Accounts SD creates sign in over ssh and nothing else, and SD
           configures the ssh server so that those accounts land in SD and
           cannot get a command prompt.  SD can only promise that about a
           server it installed and configured itself.

           SO THE INSTALLER NOW CHECKS FIRST, BEFORE IT CHANGES ANYTHING,
           and stops with an explanation if it finds:

              * another ssh server using port 22, or
              * an ssh service that is not part of Windows, installed even
                if it is not running, or
              * Windows' own ssh server with settings somebody has already
                changed.

           It names what it found - the service and where it is, or the
           setting that was added - so you can decide what to do.  Nothing on
           the computer is changed when it refuses.

           WHY IT WAS WORTH DOING.  Until now SD asked only whether Windows'
           own ssh server was present.  On a computer running a different one
           it decided there was no server at all, installed Windows' server,
           and that server could not use port 22 because the other one already
           had it.  SD's whole sign-in path was broken and the install said
           nothing.

           IF YOU HIT THIS.  Remove the other ssh server, or put this
           computer's ssh configuration back the way Windows shipped it, and
           run the installer again.

25 Aug 26  THE "Limit ssh" CHECKBOX IS GONE.  IT IS NOW A STATEMENT.

           SD limits ssh to SD users and administrators and puts every ssh
           session straight into SD.  That has been ticked by default for some
           time and was not really meant to be turned off - so showing it as a
           checkbox suggested a choice that was not one.

           IT IS NOW DESCRIBED ON THE "Before you install" page instead,
           alongside the other things SD does without asking, including the
           cost said plainly: scp and sftp stop working for everyone on the
           computer, because the command is forced and there is no subsystem
           left to run.  Remote-control tools that copy files are unaffected,
           and so are the console and Remote Desktop.

           NOTHING ABOUT THE BEHAVIOUR CHANGED - only how it is presented, and
           that it can no longer be declined.  The reason it can no longer be
           declined is the change above: the computer it existed for, the one
           with somebody else's ssh server, is now turned away before the
           install starts.

           Your existing sshd_config is still kept as sshd_config.before-sd,
           and uninstalling SD still puts it back.

25 Aug 26  SECURITY: WHEN SD INSTALLED ssh FOR YOU, PORT 22 WAS LEFT OPEN
           TO YOUR NETWORK EVEN IF YOU DID NOT ASK FOR THAT.

           SD installs the OpenSSH server whenever the machine does not
           already have one, because SD accounts sign in over ssh and
           nothing else.  Whether OTHER computers may then reach it is the
           tasks-page checkbox "Let other computers on your network connect
           to this one over ssh (port 22)", which is OFF unless you tick it.

           WHEN IT WAS OFF, THE INSTALLER WAS SUPPOSED TO NARROW THE
           FIREWALL RULE TO THIS MACHINE ONLY.  IT NEVER MANAGED TO.  The
           step failed every single time it ran, and the installer said so
           on its closing page:

              Setting who may reach ssh FAILED, and Windows' own default
              is in force - port 22 open to your local network.

           The recovery command it offered failed for the same reason, so
           there was no way to reach the intended setting.

           WHO IS AFFECTED.  Only machines where SD installed ssh itself.
           If you already had an ssh server before installing SD, SD does
           not touch its firewall rule and never did - none of this applies.

           WHAT TO DO IF YOU SAW THAT MESSAGE.  Install this build, or run
           this once from an elevated prompt:

              powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\ssh-firewall.ps1" -Installed -Restrict

           It now reports "ssh is reachable FROM THIS MACHINE ONLY".  You
           can check the setting at any time with -Show instead, or look at
           the rule "OpenSSH SSH Server (sshd)" in wf.msc.

           CONNECTING TO SD ON YOUR OWN MACHINE IS UNAFFECTED, over IPv4
           and IPv6 alike.  Windows does not filter traffic that never
           leaves the machine, so "ssh localhost" keeps working whichever
           way the setting is left.

24 Aug 26  THE "Limit ssh" WIZARD OPTION NOW WARNS FIRST, PROMISES SECOND.

           If you are installing SD on a machine that already uses ssh for
           anything - a shell for administrators, scp, sftp - the "Limit
           ssh" checkbox on the tasks page will break those uses.  It has
           always done that; the description now leads with the warning
           instead of finishing with it in a parenthesis:

              old:   Limit ssh to SD users and administrators, and put
                     every ssh session straight into SD (disables scp and
                     sftp)
              new:   DISABLES scp and sftp for everyone: puts every ssh
                     session straight into SD, and limits ssh to SD users
                     and administrators

           NOTHING ELSE CHANGES.  The task is still ticked by default; it
           can still be UNticked in the wizard; the behaviour when it is
           ticked is identical.  If sshd_config already has an AllowGroups,
           AllowUsers, DenyGroups or DenyUsers line, the install still
           refuses to touch it (allow-ssh-groups.ps1's second refusal).

24 Aug 26  SDSYS'S BP HAS BEEN TRIMMED TO ITS UTILITY PROGRAMS.

           Fifteen developer test programs that were installed alongside
           SDSYS's utility programs no longer ship - they were harmless but
           had no business being on an end user's disk.  What is left in
           SDSYS's BP is the shape the product ships with:

              PCL, PCL.GRID          printer control (Ladybridge)
              U0032, U50BB           the two user exits (0032, 50BB)
              VFS.CLS                template VFS class module

           The removed programs were BIGSTR_TEST, MSGTEST, PCODE_LIST,
           SDTEST_V8, SD_ENCRYPT, SD_ENCRYPT_B64, SD_ENCRYPT_EXT, SD_EXT,
           TEST.THEN.ELSE, TESTCRED, TESTSCRAM, TESTSDCLI, TESTSZ, pref_t,
           sdTests and tilde_test.  If you were using one of these
           deliberately - none of them are documented product functions -
           they are still in the source repository under
           sdb_ai/sd64/sdsys/bp/ up to this commit, and TESTSDCLI (kept
           because the SCRAM verifier drives it) is now at
           sdb_ai/sd64/gplbld/testsdcli.bp.

24 Aug 26  "sd <command>" NO LONGER ASKS YOU TO SET A PASSWORD.

           Running a single command - "sd COUNT VOC" - is a batch operation,
           not a session you are signing in to, so it now runs the command and
           exits instead of stopping to ask for a password.

           * NOTHING ELSE CHANGES.  Plain "sd", with no command, still asks an
             administrator whose account has no password to set one, so the
             account can be reached from the API or from another machine.
             An ssh session is unaffected: it arrives as plain "sd".
           * IT WAS ALSO A HANG.  At a console with nobody watching - a script
             or a scheduled command run in a window - the prompt had nothing to
             read and the command sat there for ever.  It now runs.
           * A scheduled task was never affected and is not changed: it has no
             terminal, so it was never asked.
           * THE INSTALLER STILL ASKS, and asks the same way it always has -
             one window, straight after setup, closing by itself when you are
             done.  It now does it by running MODIFY.PASSWORD rather than by
             relying on the login prompt, so the wording is the product's own:
             "Account <name> has no password set.  Setting the first one."
             Pressing Enter without typing one still declines, as before.


24 Aug 26  SD'S SYSTEM DIRECTORIES ARE NOW READ-ONLY TO ORDINARY SD USERS.

           Seven parts of the SD data area could be changed by anyone in the
           "sdusers" group.  They are now readable by everyone who needs them
           and writable only by an administrator:

               sdsys\accounts    the register of accounts
               sdsys\$map        sdsys\messages    sdsys\newvoc
               sdsys\bp          sdsys\cat         SD's own programs
               sd.conf           the configuration read at start-up

           * NOTHING AN ORDINARY USER DOES NEEDS TO WRITE THEM, which was
             measured on a real session before the change was made - fifteen
             commands including the spooler and saved lists, and a phantom.
           * THE COMMANDS THAT DO WRITE THEM ARE ALREADY ADMINISTRATOR
             COMMANDS and are unaffected: CREATE.ACCOUNT, DELETE.ACCOUNT,
             CATALOG and CONFIG.  Each is run from an elevated session, which
             still has full access.
           * sdsys\$ipc IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED.  Every session writes to
             it, and it is how a PHANTOM is given its command.
           * A new install applies this.  An existing installation is not
             changed, because the installer never overwrites a data area that
             is already there.  To apply it to one you already have, run this
             once from an elevated PowerShell prompt, for each of the seven:

                 powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\secure-sysdirs.ps1"
                     -Path "C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\accounts"

           This follows the same reasoning as the global catalogue and the
           pcode library, which were locked in the same way earlier.


24 Aug 26  MODIFY HAS BEEN REMOVED FROM SD CORE.

           MODIFY, the full-screen record editor inherited from OpenQM, is no
           longer part of the product.  No account of any tier receives it.
           ED remains, and is the way to edit a record.

           * MODIFY.ACCOUNT AND MODIFY.PASSWORD ARE NOT AFFECTED.  They are
             different verbs with different programs behind them, and both
             remain administrator commands.
           * A programmer account's VOC drops from 396 records to 395, and an
             administrator's from 417 to 416.  A standard account is
             unchanged at 354, because MODIFY was already withheld from it.
           * An account created before this update keeps whatever was in its
             VOC at the time - SD only ever adds records at an update, never
             removes them.


24 Aug 26  THREE MORE ERROR CODES HAVE MESSAGE TEXT, and the OPGEN
           developer tool is gone.
           * Errors 4100 (Undefined Server Error), 4101 (Invalid File
             Number) and -10303 (a SCRAM primitive failed or was given bad
             input) had no message text.  They have it now, so ERRTEXT and
             error displays name them instead of showing a bare number.
           * GPL.BP/OPGEN has been removed.  The 13 Aug entry below recorded
             that it could not be run on an installed system: it generates
             GPL.BP/OPCODES.H from the C header opcodes.h, and no install
             carries C source.  Nothing in SD calls it and no VOC pointed at
             it.  OPCODES.H is unaffected and still ships; it is regenerated
             from the repository by gplbld/gen_includes.py, which already
             produced SYSCOM/ERR.H, GPL.BP/ERRTEXT.H and GPL.BP/REVSTAMP.H.
           * Regenerating OPCODES.H dropped OP.SDPYOBJ, left behind when
             embedded Python was removed on 13 Aug.  Nothing referred to it.


24 Aug 26  THE THREE-TIER SPLIT IS SETTLED.  What each account gets on
           creation now reflects the whole split rather than a starter cut.

           STANDARD accounts get 77 verbs - what an application needs and no
           more.  Query and list (SELECT, LIST, GET.LIST and their family),
           spool and print, session and environment (LOGTO, DATE, WHO, SET),
           screen and message, prompt and input state, and eight read-only
           inspectors (SEARCH, LIST.DIFF, LIST.ITEM, LIST.COMMON, LIST.VARS,
           REPORT.SRC, REPORT.STYLE, FORMAT).  Everything an application built
           on SD invokes and nothing that edits code or data in bulk.

           PROGRAMMER accounts get all of STANDARD plus 42 more - the
           compilers, editors and cataloguer (BASIC, ED, CATALOG, RUN, MAP,
           DEBUG, MODIFY, PHANTOM, GENERATE, COMPILE.DICT and their aliases),
           the file and index definition verbs (CREATE.FILE, CONFIGURE.FILE,
           HSM, SET.TRIGGER, CREATE.INDEX and their family), the bulk record
           editors (COPY, DELETE, RENAME, REFORMAT, CNAME) and the process
           introspection verbs (PSTAT, PDEBUG, PDUMP, DUMP).

           ADMINISTRATOR accounts get everything - the 21 administration
           verbs above PROGRAMMER cover account and grant administration
           (CREATE.ACCOUNT, GRANT, UNLOCK, MODIFY.PASSWORD, ENCRYPT.FIELD and
           their family), system-wide state (CONFIG, LISTU, LIST.LOCKS,
           CLEAR.LOCKS, LOGOUT, SET.DATE), and the shell escapes (SH, !).

           WHAT MOVED SINCE THE OWNER'S FIRST-PASS CUT.  The debug family
           (PSTAT, PDEBUG, PDUMP, DUMP) moved from ADMINISTRATOR to
           PROGRAMMER - a programmer needs these to debug their own code.
           The read-only inspectors moved from PROGRAMMER to STANDARD - they
           read only, they need no BASIC or catalogue, an application can
           invoke them.

           UMASK WAS REMOVED FROM EVERY TIER.  UMASK controls POSIX file-mode
           bits, which Windows does not use for security - all file access
           runs through the ACLs an SD account already receives.  Setting a
           umask on Windows produces no security effect and would only be
           misleading.  The record types stay compiled but no VOC in any tier
           dispatches to them.

           TWELVE VERB RECORDS LEFT NEWVOC.  config, listu, list.readu,
           list.locks, clear.locks, lock, logout, set.date, sh, !,
           clean.account and umask are no longer copied into any account's
           VOC.  Administrators still receive them, from VOC_TEMPLATE, via
           TIER.ADD.ADMINISTRATOR.  A programmer or standard account created
           before this update keeps whichever of them was in its VOC then -
           SD only ever adds records at an update, never removes them.


24 Aug 26  SD NOW WRITES WINDOWS LINE ENDINGS TO FILES YOU CAN OPEN.

           Anything SD writes that an ordinary Windows program can open now
           ends its lines the way Windows does, with CR+LF.  That covers
           records in a directory file, WRITESEQ and WRITECSV output, command
           output captured with COMO, printer output sent to a file, and the
           error log.

           WHY.  A directory file exists so that its records ARE ordinary
           files - you can open one in Notepad, or point Excel at a CSV SD
           produced.  Those files were being written with the Unix line
           ending, so Notepad ran the lines together and a CSV was not quite
           the format it claimed to be.  SD's CSV statements are documented as
           following the RFC 4180 standard, and that standard asks for CR+LF.

           WHAT IS NOT AFFECTED.  Dynamic files are stored in SD's own format
           and are not readable by other programs, so nothing about them
           changes.  Nothing about how SD reads files changes either - it
           accepts both endings, as the entry below describes.

           EXISTING FILES ARE LEFT ALONE.  Only newly written data uses the
           new ending, so a file can contain both.  SD reads either, so this
           is untidy rather than a problem.


24 Aug 26  FILES EDITED IN NOTEPAD, OR SAVED FROM EXCEL, NOW READ CORRECTLY.

           Directory files exist so that you can edit their records with an
           ordinary Windows editor.  Windows editors end each line with CR+LF.
           SD only ever looked for the LF, so it kept the CR - and put it on
           the end of the data.

           You would have seen it as an invisible extra character at the end
           of every line: comparisons failing for no visible reason, a name
           that would not match, a trailing space that was not a space.

           WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE.  Reading a CSV file saved by Excel is
           the clearest case.  The last column of every row picked up that
           stray character, because a comma ended the other columns and the
           line ending only ever touched the last one.  READCSV, READSEQ and
           reading a directory file record are all corrected.

           A CR ON ITS OWN IS STILL DATA and is left exactly as it is.  Only
           the CR+LF pair that ends a line is treated as a line ending, so
           this cannot alter data that happens to contain a CR.

           Files SD itself writes are unchanged for now - it still writes LF.
           Reading was the half that lost data, so it is the half fixed here.


24 Aug 26  AN API SESSION NOW KEEPS YOUR IDENTITY WHEN IT OPENS AND WRITES
           FILES.  This closes the gap described in the 23 Aug entry below.

           WHAT WAS WRONG.  Taking on your Windows identity applied only to the
           one thread that did it.  While switching to your account the session
           starts a short-lived helper process, and starting it quietly put the
           session back to the service's own identity - with no error and
           nothing in the log - before it had opened a single file of yours.
           So the login half worked and the part you would actually notice did
           not: records came out owned by the system account rather than by you.

           WHAT CHANGES FOR YOU.  Records an API session creates are now owned
           by the account that logged in, and the session reaches files with
           your access rather than the service's.  If your account may not read
           something, the API session may not read it either.

           IF YOUR IDENTITY CANNOT BE TAKEN ON, THE LOGIN IS REFUSED rather
           than continued with the service's identity.  A session that believes
           it is you while holding the service's rights is worse than one that
           never started.

           The error-log alarm in the entry directly below stays as it is.  A
           healthy session writes nothing to it, so silence there is now the
           normal case rather than a sign the alarm is untested.


24 Aug 26  THE ERROR LOG NOW SAYS IF AN API SESSION LOSES YOUR IDENTITY.

           The entry below for 23 Aug explains that an API session takes on
           your Windows identity when you log in, but does not yet use it when
           it opens files.  This adds the missing alarm for that.

           If the session ever believes it is you while Windows says it is not,
           a line is written to the error log naming where it happened:

             API IDENTITY LOST at record write - session believes it is ...

           Nothing is written when the two agree, so a healthy session is
           silent and you will not see this at all once the underlying problem
           is fixed.  It changes no behaviour: nothing is refused or slowed
           because of it.  It is there so the condition cannot go unnoticed
           again, which is how it went unnoticed in the first place.


24 Aug 26  THE SIGN-ON BANNER NOW SAYS "SD CORE".

           The line printed when you sign on reads "SD Core for Windows, the
           Essential Multivalue String Database".  It is a name change only -
           nothing about what the product does has changed with it.  The point
           is to tell this build apart from regular SD at a glance, so that a
           screenshot or a support question says which one it came from.


23 Aug 26  AN API SESSION TAKES YOUR WINDOWS IDENTITY AT LOGIN, BUT DOES NOT
           YET USE IT FOR FILES.  Revised 24 Aug 26.

           This entry first said an API session runs as the user who logged in.
           Measuring it on 24 Aug showed that only the login half of that is
           true, and the file half - the half you would actually notice - is
           not.  What follows is what happens today.

           WHAT WORKS.  After a successful login the session takes on your
           Windows identity.  No password is involved: the server never has one
           to use, and the identity is granted by Windows on the service's own
           privilege.  If that step fails the login fails - you are told that
           authentication succeeded but the session could not take your Windows
           identity, and the connection is refused.  It is deliberately not
           allowed to carry on with the service's rights instead.

           WHAT DOES NOT.  That identity does not reach the files.  A record
           written by an API session is still owned by the account the SD
           service runs as, not by you, and files the session opens are still
           opened with the service's rights.  So do not rely on file ownership,
           or on per-user file permissions, to tell one API user from another
           yet.  This is being worked on.

           WHAT IS UNAFFECTED.  A session can still only reach the accounts the
           caller is entitled to.  That containment is a separate mechanism and
           it has been checked.  And sessions reached at the keyboard or over
           ssh were always the user already - only the API path is in question
           here.

23 Aug 26  SD CANNOT BE INSTALLED SILENTLY ANY MORE.

           /SILENT and /VERYSILENT are now refused, with a message saying why.
           There is no switch to override it.

           Installing ends by asking you for a password, and a silent install
           has nobody to ask, so it used to finish with no password set for any
           account - and said nothing about it.  The result looked like a
           complete installation but could not be reached over ssh or through
           the SD API at all, and could be used only at that computer from a
           session run as administrator.

           Unattended deployment is not supported.  Install at the computer's
           keyboard, or through Remote Desktop or similar remote-control
           software - both work, because a person is there to answer.  Nothing
           changes for a normal install.

23 Aug 26  YOU ARE NOW TOLD WHAT SETTING NO PASSWORD ACTUALLY COSTS.

           Both the end of the installer and SD's own prompt used to say that
           without a password you could not use ssh or the API, and stopped
           there.  That was true and easy to read as "some things will not
           work".

           It is stronger than that, and both now say so: with no password the
           account can be used ONLY at that computer - at the keyboard, or
           through Remote Desktop or similar remote-control software - and only
           from a session run as administrator.

           You can still choose it, and SD still asks again the next time you
           open the account.

23 Aug 26  THE PCODE LIBRARY IS NO LONGER WRITABLE BY SD USERS.

           <sysdir>\bin holds the pcode library - the interpreter itself,
           which SD loads into shared memory when it starts and every session
           then runs.  Until now any member of the sdusers group could write
           to it, so one SD user could have replaced what everybody else's
           session executes, including an administrator's.

           That directory is now readable by SD users and writable only by
           administrators, which is the same protection the global catalogue
           has had since 18 Aug.  Nothing needs to write it after an install:
           only the process that starts SD reads it, and that is already
           elevated.

           If you have anything that writes into <sysdir>\bin, it will now be
           refused and will need to run elevated.

23 Aug 26  THE MESSAGE AT THE END OF INSTALLATION IS EASIER TO READ.

           It said the same things, in one tall narrow column that ran most of
           the height of the screen.  It is now wider and 40% shorter.

           It also names your SD account the way the rest of SD does.  It used
           to print the name in capitals - "named DON" - where LIST ACCOUNTS
           and the account's own directory both say "don".

23 Aug 26  PROC, SED AND UPDATE.RECORD HAVE BEEN REMOVED.

           SED was the full-screen editor and UPDATE.RECORD the full-screen
           record editor.  Both are gone, along with the PROC language: a VOC
           item of type PQ now reports that PROC is not supported instead of
           running.  Your PQ records are left alone - it is the interpreter
           that has gone, not the records.

           ED, the line editor, is not affected and is the editor this system
           uses.  Neither is anything you run with LIST, COUNT, SELECT or
           SORT: those are the query processor, which is a different thing
           from PROC despite the similar name.

22 Aug 26  A SCHEDULED JOB CAN NOW RUN AN SD COMMAND, AND ONLY THE COMMANDS
           AN ADMINISTRATOR HAS NAMED FOR IT.

           Until now, typing a command after "sd" - "sd MY.REPORT" - needed a
           session started with "Run as administrator".  A scheduled task does
           not run that way, so there was no way to have SD do anything on a
           timer without handing the job administrator rights.

           Now there are two ways past that.  An elevated session still runs
           anything, exactly as before.  Anything else runs what is listed for
           its account in the SD system file batch.jobs, which only an
           administrator can change: one record per account, one command name
           on each line.

           The command must be a single name with nothing after it, and it
           must be a paragraph or a sentence in that account.  There is no
           password anywhere in this - the job signs in as its own Windows
           account and SD puts it in the matching SD account.

           Nothing changes for a session you type at: commands entered at the
           ":" prompt are unaffected.

22 Aug 26  A WINDOWS PATH TYPED AT THE COMMAND PROMPT IS NO LONGER CUT OFF
           AT THE FIRST BACKSLASH.

           Typing a path such as C:\Data\Sales was read as just "C:".  The
           rest was treated as though you had typed a second, separate thing.
           CREATE.ACCOUNT OTHER, which is given a folder to put the account
           in, was the command most likely to show it: the account went to
           the wrong place, or the command failed for a reason that made no
           sense from what you had typed.

           Typing the same path with forward slashes always worked, and
           still does.  Both are now read the same way.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLATION CHECK NO LONGER STOPS WITH "Access is denied"
           WHEN YOU RUN IT BEFORE SIGNING OUT.

           The installer puts you in the "sdusers" group, and Windows only
           applies that when you next sign in - so on the sign-in that ran
           the installer, the database cannot be read yet.  The check knows
           about this and is meant to say so, in as many words, and tell you
           to sign out and back in.

           Instead it stopped dead with "Access is denied" and told you
           nothing, because looking for a folder it is not allowed to read
           raises an error rather than simply answering "no".  That was the
           one case the check exists to handle kindly, and it was the only
           case it had never been run in.

           It now says the database could not be read on this sign-in, and
           that this is the same membership it has just told you about
           rather than a second fault.

           For the same reason it no longer claims the network API is
           switched off when what actually happened is that it could not
           read the settings file.

22 Aug 26  SD NO LONGER LOSES TRACK OF WHAT YOUR TERMINAL CAN DO WHILE YOU
           ARE LOGGING IN.

           SD reads your terminal's abilities from a description filed
           under the name in your TERM setting.  If there was no
           description of that name - and there is none for
           xterm-256color, which is what a terminal usually reports here
           even when you have not set TERM at all - SD carried on with no
           terminal abilities whatsoever until the account's login
           paragraph set the type a second time.

           That happened a moment too late.  Everything the login sequence
           itself does came before it, so the screen was not cleared when
           you signed on.

           This is the reason behind the Backspace fault listed below, and
           that fault was already repaired separately - the password prompt
           no longer depends on the terminal description at all, and works
           either way.

           SD now checks that the description it asked for was actually
           found, and falls back to  windows  when it was not.

           If you have set TERM to a name SD does have - windows, xterm,
           linux, ansi and about sixty others are supplied - nothing
           changes for you.

22 Aug 26  ACCOUNT NAMES ARE NOW LOWER CASE ON DISK.

           SD keeps one file per account in sdsys\accounts, and that file
           used to be named in capitals - DON - while everything else
           belonging to the same account was lower case, including its own
           directory in user_accounts.  New accounts are now recorded in
           lower case, so the two match.

           NOTHING YOU TYPE CHANGES.  Account names have never been case
           sensitive and still are not: create.account, logto and the rest
           accept whatever case you type, and existing accounts keep the
           names they already have.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLATION CHECK CLOSES ON A KEYPRESS.

           Its window used to stay open at a PowerShell prompt, so the only
           way to be rid of it was to know to type  exit  there.  It now
           ends with "Press any key to close this window" and closes.

22 Aug 26  YOU NO LONGER HAVE TO TYPE  off  AFTER SETTING YOUR PASSWORD.

           The window the installer opens closes SD by itself once the
           password is set, and goes straight on to checking the install.

           The installer's last page now also says that this window is
           going to open, so a console appearing after Setup has closed is
           not a surprise.

           If no password is set - because you pressed Enter to skip it -
           the window says so, and says that SD will ask again the first
           time you open the account.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NOW FINISHES BEFORE IT ASKS YOU ANYTHING ELSE.

           The installer used to open SD for your password while its own
           window was still on screen, and then offer the installation
           check as a tick box on its last page - so one action asked you
           twice, once on that page and again in the check itself.

           Now the installer closes first.  A single window then opens and
           does two things in turn: SD asks for your account password, and
           when you type  off  the same window checks the installation.

           The tick box is gone.  The check still asks before it runs, so
           you can decline it, and it is on the Start Menu whenever you
           want it: SD > Check the SD installation.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLATION CHECK NOW SAYS WHEN IT HAS FINISHED.

           Its window stays open on purpose, so that you can read what it
           found - but nothing told you the work was over, which left you
           wondering whether it still wanted something.  It now ends with
           "Test completed - you can close this window now."

22 Aug 26  BACKSPACE NOW WORKS WHEN YOU ARE ASKED FOR A PASSWORD.

           Typing a password at an SD prompt - the one the installer asks
           for, and any other - ignored the Backspace key, so a mistake
           could not be corrected.  It was worse than it looked: the key
           was added to what you had typed rather than removed from it, so
           trying to correct a slip made the password longer.

           Both kinds of Backspace key now erase, whichever your terminal
           sends.

           The same prompt now explains itself.  It says what the password
           is for - you need it only when connecting from another computer,
           over ssh or the API, never at this keyboard - and it tells you to
           type  off  when you have finished.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER CAN NOW CHECK ITSELF WHEN IT FINISHES.

           The last page of the installer has a new tickbox, on by default:
           "Check that SD installed correctly".  Leave it ticked and a window
           opens listing what was checked - the program files, the SD service,
           your access to the database, whether SD's programs were built, and
           the network options if you turned them on.

           IT ONLY LOOKS.  It changes nothing at all, so you can run it as
           often as you like.  There is now a Start Menu entry for it as well:
           SD > Check the SD installation.

           SOME CHECKS WILL SAY "not yet", AND THAT IS NORMAL.  The installer
           puts you in a group called sdusers, which is what lets you open the
           database - but Windows only works out which groups you are in when
           you sign in, so it does not take effect until you sign out and back
           in.  Until you do, the check cannot read the database and says so
           instead of pretending something is broken.  Sign out, sign back in,
           and run it again from the Start Menu.

           If you run it as an administrator it will tell you that the answers
           are about the administrator account rather than about you.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NO LONGER ASKS WHERE TO PUT SD.

           SD installs to C:\Program Files\SD and its database to
           C:\ProgramData\SD.  The wizard used to let you change the first
           of those but never the second, which meant the two could end up
           apart and only half the install had moved.

           The page that asked has been removed, so both now go where they
           have always been documented to go.  If you installed an earlier
           version somewhere else, this one will not follow it there.

           Nothing you do day to day changes.

22 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NOW GIVES YOU AN SD ACCOUNT ON TURKISH AND AZERI
           SYSTEMS TOO.

           The installer makes an SD account for whoever authorises it, and to
           do that it has to match your Windows user name against SD's copy of
           it - which means changing both to the same case.  Windows and SD do
           not change case the same way everywhere: on a Turkish or Azeri
           system Windows turns "I" into a dotless "i", and SD does not.  A
           user name containing that letter did not match itself, so the
           install finished without giving you an SD account.

           Both sides now use the same rule, which does not vary by locale.
           Nothing changes on a system whose locale was never affected.

21 Aug 26  FAILED API LOGINS ARE NOW WRITTEN TO THE AUDIT TRAIL.

           Until now the audit file recorded only one kind of API refusal: an
           account that authenticated correctly but had not been given API
           access.  Everything else - a wrong password, an account with no API
           password set, a password set before the SCRAM change, a user name
           the server will not accept - was refused and left no trace at all.
           If you were asked "who has been trying to log in?", there was
           nothing to answer with.

           Every refusal is recorded now, in sdsys\audit, one line each:

               API REFUSED user=jim reason=wrong password

           The reasons are plain: wrong password, no credential, credential is
           version 1 - run MODIFY.PASSWORD, name rejected by valid_os_name,
           not in sdapi, and a few for malformed or out-of-order messages.

           WHAT THE CLIENT SEES HAS NOT CHANGED.  It is still told the same
           thing for every credential failure - "Invalid username or password"
           - after the same pause.  The detail goes only to the audit file,
           which ordinary SD accounts can add to but cannot read.

           A user name is recorded as it was typed, except that anything
           outside letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen becomes "?",
           and a long one is cut to 32 characters and marked "...".  So
           GITORLI\jim is recorded as GITORLI?jim.  That is deliberate: the
           name has not been checked at that point, and a name containing a
           new line could otherwise forge a line in the trail.

           Note that anyone who can reach the API port can now cause a line to
           be written, at most one per connection.  If the file grows more
           than you want, it is safe to move it aside; SD starts a new one.

21 Aug 26  THE INSTALLER NOW FINISHES BY OPENING SD, SO YOU CAN GIVE YOUR OWN
           ACCOUNT A PASSWORD.

           When you close the installer's last dialog, a console window opens,
           already logged in to the SD account the installer has just made for
           you, and asks you to set a password for it.  Then it leaves you at
           the prompt like any other session; type "off" when you have
           finished.

           It can run before you sign out and back in, which the same dialog
           tells you is needed, because it borrows the installer's rights.
           Your own "sd" still has to wait for the sign-out.

           THAT PASSWORD IS NOT FOR THIS CONSOLE.  Typing "sd" on the machine
           itself has never asked for one and still does not - Windows has
           already established who you are.  The password is what reaches the
           account from SOMEWHERE ELSE: the API, and anything else that has to
           prove who it is over a network.  Without one, the account works
           perfectly here and cannot be reached from anywhere.

           You can close the window without setting one.  SD will ask again the
           next time you open it - see the entry below.  An unattended
           (/VERYSILENT) install never opens the window at all, for the obvious
           reason that there is nobody there; those accounts are asked the
           first time somebody uses them.

21 Aug 26  AN ACCOUNT WITH NO PASSWORD IS ASKED TO SET ONE WHEN IT IS OPENED.

           If an SD account has no password, opening it from an ELEVATED
           console asks for one before you reach the prompt, and the session
           ends if you decline.  It is the same two hidden prompts
           modify.password uses.

           WHO THIS CAN HAPPEN TO IS A SHORT LIST.  Every account created since
           the previous release has a password from the moment it is made, so
           in practice this is the account the installer gives the person
           installing SD, and anything left over from an older version.

           IT ONLY ASKS WHERE YOU CAN ANSWER.  Not in an ordinary
           (unelevated) session - the credential store is readable only by
           administrators, so an ordinary session could not set one anyway, and
           it is not asked to.  Not when SD is reading a piped script, which
           has no one at the keyboard.  Not for a group account, which has no
           password by design.

21 Aug 26  A FAILED create.account NOW REPORTS FAILURE TO A SCRIPT.

           Only affects you if you TEST the result in a PROC or a paragraph.
           Nothing about what you see on screen changes.

           When create.account could not create the Windows account it set
           @SYSTEM.RETURN.CODE to 6 instead of -6.  Error codes there are
           negative; positive values mean "this many things were done", which
           is how copy and delete report their counts.  So a PROC asking the
           usual "if @system.return.code < 0" was told the account had been
           created when it had not, and carried on to the next name.  The
           message on screen said otherwise the whole time.

           The same missing minus is fixed in ed, which preset its
           command-error code the same way.

21 Aug 26  ACCOUNTS SAY HOW THEY ARE REACHED, AND set.password IS NOW
           modify.password.  Five changes to the account verbs.

           1. CREATE.ACCOUNT USER NEEDS TO BE TOLD ABOUT REMOTE ACCESS.

                  create.account user <name> ssh
                  create.account user <name> api
                  create.account user <name> both
                  create.account user <name> none

              One of the four is REQUIRED.  There is no default: an account
              that should only ever be reached with LOGTO says "none" and
              means it, and the old silent "ssh yes, API no" could not tell
              that apart from somebody who had not thought about it.

              An ADMINISTRATOR account always gets both and needs no keyword.
              GROUP accounts take none of this - they have no Windows account
              and are reached with LOGTO or an F pointer.

           2. MODIFY.ACCOUNT TAKES THE SAME FOUR WORDS, and they REPLACE the
              old SSH / NO.SSH / API / NO.API pairs.

              THE KEYWORD NOW SAYS WHAT THE ACCESS IS, NOT WHAT TO ADD.  So

                  modify.account fred api

              gives Fred the API AND TAKES ssh AWAY.  If you want both, say
              "both".  The message afterwards always names both routes, so you
              can see what you have left him with.

              It refuses on an administrator's account: administrators always
              have both.

           3. ADMINISTRATORS CAN USE THE API.  Until now they could not - an
              administrator's account joined neither group, and the API
              requires membership, so it refused them.  Nothing you need to do;
              accounts created from now on are correct.

           4. DELETE.ACCOUNT ASKS ONCE, THEN REMOVES EVERYTHING.  It used to
              ask only about the account directory and remove the rest without
              asking.  The single question names exactly what will go,
              including the Windows account when there is one to remove.

              IT STILL WILL NOT DELETE A WINDOWS ACCOUNT SD DID NOT CREATE.
              That is unchanged, and the question uses shorter wording in that
              case rather than promising something it will not do.

           5. set.password IS NOW modify.password.  Same verb, same
              behaviour - the name changed because every account now has a
              password from the moment it is made, so there is nothing to
              "set" for the first time.  A user account cannot be created
              without one: refusing the prompt now creates nothing at all,
              where before it left an account you could not sign in to.

21 Aug 26  THE API IS REACHED AT ITS OWN PORT NOW, not through an ssh tunnel.

           If you connect a client with something like
           "ssh -L 4243:127.0.0.1:4243 user@host" you no longer need to:
           point the client straight at port 4243 on the server instead.
           The tunnel still works, but it is no longer what the design
           expects and it is not tested.

           WHAT CHANGED, in three parts:

           * sd.conf now sets APIPORT=4243.  It used to ship commented
             out, so the API was off until you turned it on.
           * The server accepts API connections on any network interface.
             It used to listen on 127.0.0.1 only, which is why a tunnel
             was needed.
           * Installation offers "Let other computers on your network
             connect to the SD API (port 4243)", TICKED BY DEFAULT.  Untick
             it and the port is reachable from the machine itself only.

           WHAT STILL STANDS BETWEEN A STRANGER AND YOUR DATA.  Reaching
           the port is not the same as getting in.  A caller must:

           * complete a SCRAM-SHA-256 exchange against a password held for
             that account - so an account with no password cannot connect
             at all;
           * be a member of the "sdapi" group, which an account joins
             only when you give it API access; and
           * pass the account's own group check.

           A session that does get in is confined to the account it signed
           in to.  It cannot open SD's credential store and cannot run
           operating-system commands.

           TO GO BACK TO LOCAL-ONLY at any time, from an elevated prompt:

               powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\api-firewall.ps1" -Restrict

           and to turn the API off altogether, comment out the APIPORT line
           in C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf and restart SD.

           Uninstalling SD removes the firewall rule it created.

21 Aug 26  "Limit ssh to SD users and administrators" is now ticked by
           default during installation.  It was offered unticked before.

           WHAT IT DOES, and it is worth reading before you accept it:

           * Only members of "sdssh" and the administrators group may ssh in.
           * Every ssh session goes straight into SD rather than to a shell.
           * BECAUSE OF THAT SECOND ONE, scp AND sftp STOP WORKING for
             everyone on the machine.  If you copy files over ssh, untick it.
           * The original sshd_config is kept as sshd_config.before-sd.

           * IT REFUSES BY ITSELF IF YOUR sshd_config ALREADY SAYS WHO MAY
             CONNECT.  An existing AllowGroups, AllowUsers, DenyGroups or
             DenyUsers line is somebody's policy, and SD will not widen it,
             replace it or merge into it - it leaves the file alone and tells
             you so.
           * AN UNATTENDED INSTALL NOW APPLIES IT TOO, where before it applied
             nothing.  If you install SD silently onto machines with ssh
             servers you manage separately, this is the change to look at.

           You can untick it during installation, and you can undo it
           afterwards with:

              powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\allow-ssh-groups.ps1" -Remove

21 Aug 26  DELETE.ACCOUNT now removes the Windows account as part of the job,
           instead of stopping to ask, and it removes that account's Windows
           profile with it.

           * IT ONLY EVER REMOVES AN ACCOUNT SD CREATED.  If the Windows
             account was already there when CREATE.ACCOUNT was run - which is
             the ordinary case, since an SD account is normally named after a
             Windows user who already exists - it is left alone and
             DELETE.ACCOUNT says so.  That check is what protects a borrowed
             login, and it has not changed.
           * WHY THE PROMPT WENT: not deleting was not the quiet outcome.  It
             left a Windows account and its profile behind with nothing
             pointing at them and nothing ever coming back for them.
           * THE PROFILE MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS.  Deleting only the
             C:\Users\<name> folder leaves a registry entry behind, and the
             next account of the same name then gets its profile put in
             C:\Users\<name>.<COMPUTERNAME> instead.  Both halves go now.
           * THE ACCOUNT'S OWN DIRECTORY IS STILL ASKED ABOUT SEPARATELY, as
             before.  That is your data; this is the login SD made to reach it.
           * IF THE PROFILE CANNOT BE REMOVED - someone is signed in as that
             user, or a file is open - the account is still deleted and you are
             told the profile was left behind.

21 Aug 26  A session that arrives over the API is now confined to the account
           it is standing in.  Sessions at the keyboard and over ssh are not
           affected in any way.

           WHY: until this version, a client connecting over the API could open
           any file on this machine - including the file SD keeps passwords in.
           Holding one ordinary account's password was enough, and no
           administration command was needed.  If you have used the API, treat
           the passwords of every account as having been reachable.

           * WHAT AN API SESSION CAN STILL DO: everything inside its own
             account, and the shipped SDSYS files every account needs -
             messages, syscom, the dictionaries, sd.voclib.  Ordinary programs
             are not affected.
           * WHAT IT CAN NO LONGER DO: open, rename, delete or list anything
             else.  A refused OPEN takes the ELSE branch and STATUS() is 3035,
             which means "not permitted" rather than "not found".
           * IF YOUR DATA LIVES OUTSIDE AN ACCOUNT - a shared directory that
             VOC entries point at - name it in the new NETDIRS setting in
             C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf, separating several with a semicolon.
             Nothing else needs changing.  "CONFIG('NETDIRS')" prints what is
             in force.
           * THE PASSWORD FILE, THE PROGRAM CATALOGUE AND THE ACCOUNT REGISTER
             ARE NEVER REACHABLE from an API session, and cannot be added to
             NETDIRS.

21 Aug 26  OS.EXECUTE and SH are no longer available to a session that arrived
           over the API.

           They were, and they ran as the LocalSystem account - so a remote
           client could run any command on the machine with full privilege,
           which is more than the administrator sitting at the keyboard gets.
           Measured from inside such a session on 21 Aug 2026.  An API session
           is no longer treated as an administrator for any purpose, which is
           what SD's own code already assumed and did not enforce.

21 Aug 26  "Limit ssh to SD users and administrators" is now offered on every
           install, not only on a machine that had no ssh server beforehand.

           It is still unticked by default and still does nothing unless you
           tick it.  Before this, SD's own first install put an ssh server on
           the machine permanently, so the option was never offered again -
           while uninstalling always removed the setting.  The result was that
           the restriction could be applied once and never re-applied, and on
           this machine it had silently lapsed.

           * IF YOU HAVE UPGRADED BEFORE AND USE ssh, check whether the
             restriction is still in place; it may have been removed by an
             earlier uninstall.  To put it back:

                powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\allow-ssh-groups.ps1" -Installed

           * IT WILL REFUSE if your sshd_config already says who may connect,
             rather than overwrite somebody else's policy.
           * TICKING IT DISABLES scp AND sftp for everyone on the machine,
             which is why it is an option and not a default.

21 Aug 26  You can now say, per account, whether it may reach this machine over
           ssh, through an API client, both, or neither.  Two new
           MODIFY.ACCOUNT actions, from SDSYS:

              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH        may sign in over ssh
              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NO.SSH     may not
              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API        may use the API
              MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NO.API     may not

           * A NEW ACCOUNT GETS ssh AND NOT THE API.  That is a change: until
             now every account could use the API as soon as the port was open.
             Say "MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API" for each account that needs it.
           * WHY THE API STARTS OFF: an API session currently runs with more
             privilege than the person connecting - see the entry below, dated
             20 Aug.  Until that is fixed, an account should only be able to
             use the API because somebody decided it should.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP ssh AND LOSE NOTHING ELSE.  Installing this
             version copies everyone who could ssh in before into the new group
             that now controls it, so nobody is locked out.  Nobody is put into
             the API group, so if you were using the API, grant it again to the
             accounts that need it.
           * BOTH TAKE EFFECT AT THE NEXT CONNECTION.  There is no need to sign
             out of Windows, and no need to restart SD.
           * "LIST.GRANTS" is unrelated and unchanged - that answers "who may
             use this account", which is a different question from "how may
             this person reach the machine".

21 Aug 26  RDPACCOUNT is gone, and with it NO.RDPACCOUNT.

           * WHAT IT DID: it let an account created by SD sign in to Windows.
             It was named for Remote Desktop but it did not stop there - the
             same Windows setting covers Remote Desktop and the physical
             keyboard together, so an account created with RDPACCOUNT could
             also walk up to the machine and log in.  That was never the
             intention.
           * THE RULE IT GOT IN THE WAY OF, and which now holds without
             exception: NOBODY SD CREATES CAN LOG IN TO WINDOWS AT THIS
             MACHINE UNLESS THEY ARE ALREADY A WINDOWS ADMINISTRATOR.  SD
             accounts reach the machine over ssh or through an API client.
           * If you type RDPACCOUNT now, CREATE.ACCOUNT stops with
             "Unexpected token (RDPACCOUNT)" and makes no account.
           * ACCOUNTS ALREADY CREATED WITH IT KEEP THEIR WINDOWS SIGN-IN.
             Nothing goes round and takes it back, because SD did not record
             which accounts they were.  If you have any, either delete and
             recreate them, or take them out of the Windows group by hand:
                 net localgroup sdsshonly <name> /add
           * If you want Remote Desktop, you want Windows Server and RDP
             client access licences, and probably a commercial product built
             for it.  It is outside what this port is for.

20 Aug 26  The API port is OFF by default again, and you should leave it off
           for now.  This reverses the entry below it, made earlier the same
           day.
           * APIPORT is commented out in C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf on a new
             install.  Uncomment it to switch the API on.
           * WHY, PLAINLY: an API session currently runs with more privilege
             than the person connecting.  SD's background service runs as
             LocalSystem and starts each API session itself, so the session
             inherits the service's privileges instead of the client's.  A
             programmer-level account connecting over the API was measured
             reading and writing SD's password store, which no ordinary user
             can touch.
           * WHAT THAT MEANS FOR YOU.  If you switch the API on, treat anyone
             with an API password on a programmer or administrator account as
             having full control of the machine.  Standard accounts appear far
             more limited, but that has not been proved and should not be
             relied on.
           * Turning the port off is enough to avoid it.  Nothing else about
             SD is affected, and terminal sessions are not affected at all -
             they run as you, which is what makes them safe.
           * This will be fixed and the port will go back to being on by
             default.  Until then the default errs the other way.

20 Aug 26  The error log now records who connects to the API port.
           * Every accepted API (SDClient) connection adds a line to
             C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\errlog naming the Windows process and
             account at the other end:
                 API connection from 127.0.0.1:59314 - pid 11448, GITORLI\don
           * NOTHING IS REFUSED ON THE STRENGTH OF IT.  This records who
             connected; it does not decide who may.  The API's own checks are
             unchanged - the port is loopback only, a client must complete the
             SCRAM exchange against a stored credential, and the account must
             pass the group check.
           * A CONNECTION FORWARDED OVER ssh SHOWS sshd, not the person at the
             far end.  The tunnel ends on this machine, so the process that
             connects genuinely is sshd.  What the line distinguishes is a
             client running ON this machine from one arriving through a
             tunnel; it cannot name a remote person.
           * "peer process not identified" means the client had already gone
             by the time the connection was looked up.  It is not an error and
             the connection proceeds normally.
           * ERRLOG NOW APPLIES TO THESE LINES TOO.  The background daemon
             used to append to the error log without ever trimming it - it
             only wrote at startup and on failure, so it never grew.  Now that
             it writes per connection, it discards the oldest part of the log
             on reaching the ERRLOG size in sd.conf, exactly as SD sessions
             have always done.  If you have set ERRLOG unusually large,
             consider what an entry per connection adds to it.
           * AFTER THE LOG IS TRIMMED, ITS FIRST LINE MAY HAVE NO TIMESTAMP.
             An entry is two lines - a timestamped header and the message
             indented below it - and trimming restarts the file at a line,
             not at an entry, so the first message can be left without its
             header.  This is not damage and no entry after it is affected.
             SD has always trimmed the log this way; it is only visible now
             because the log turns over more often.

20 Aug 26  A new kind of account: RDPACCOUNT, which may sign in to Windows.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER jim RDPACCOUNT
             CREATE.ACCOUNT USER jim PROGRAMMER RDPACCOUNT
             The account may sign in at the console, over Remote Desktop, and
             run other Windows programs - as well as over ssh.  Without the
             keyword an account is confined to ssh, which is unchanged and
             stays the default.
           * IT IS NOT A FOURTH TIER.  It is orthogonal to STANDARD,
             PROGRAMMER and ADMINISTRATOR: give it with any of them, in either
             order.  The tier decides what the account can DO inside SD; this
             decides how the person reaches the machine.
           * AN EXISTING ACCOUNT CAN BE MOVED EITHER WAY:
                 MODIFY.ACCOUNT JIM RDPACCOUNT      may sign in to Windows
                 MODIFY.ACCOUNT JIM NO.RDPACCOUNT   confined to ssh again
           * AN ADMINISTRATOR CANNOT BE CONFINED TO ssh, and NO.RDPACCOUNT
             refuses rather than doing it.  Confining an administrator would
             deny them console and Remote Desktop sign-in, and they would be
             unable to get back in after the session they typed it in.
           * WHAT IT COSTS YOU TO KNOW.  An RDPACCOUNT user has a desktop, so
             they can reach files with Explorer and run programs SD does not
             control.  Each account's own directory is locked to that account
             (see the entry below), which is what makes this safe to offer;
             but it is a wider kind of access than an ssh-only account has,
             and it is not the default for that reason.

20 Aug 26  The API port is now open by default.
           * APIPORT=4243 is set in C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf on a new
             install, instead of being commented out for you to switch on.
             SD listens for API (SDClient) connections as soon as it starts.
           * IT LISTENS ON 127.0.0.1 ONLY, never on a network interface, so
             nothing off this machine can reach it.  A client on another
             machine still forwards the port over ssh:
                 ssh -L 4243:127.0.0.1:4243 <user>@<host>
           * AN OPEN PORT IS NOT AN OPEN DOOR.  A client still has to complete
             the SCRAM login against a password held for that account, and
             then pass the account's group check.  No account has an API
             password until you run SET.PASSWORD for it, and until then every
             login attempt is refused.
           * WHY IT CHANGED.  Until 20 Aug the API login sent your password in
             clear, so opening the port by default would have been careless.
             That login is gone - see the entry below - and the reason for the
             cautious default went with it.  Installing also deletes and
             rebuilds the data tree, so the old default meant switching the
             port back on by hand after every upgrade.
           * TO TURN IT OFF, comment the APIPORT line out and restart SD.  No
             port is then opened.  The setting is read when SD starts, not
             when a session begins.

20 Aug 26  Your account directory is locked to you, and the installer now
           finishes the job on every account instead of stopping at the first
           awkward one.
           * WHAT THE LOCK IS.  C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts\<name> is
             granted to that account's own group, to administrators and to the
             system, and to nobody else.  Before it, every SD user could read
             and rewrite every other user's account files from Explorer or a
             command prompt - outside SD, so none of SD's own permission
             checks applied.  SD itself has always refused to let you LOGTO an
             account you are not a member of; this makes the files agree with
             that.
           * NOTHING YOU TYPE CHANGES, and your own account is unaffected -
             you are a member of its group.
           * THE FIX.  The installer re-applies the lock to accounts that
             already existed, and it used to STOP at the first account whose
             Windows group had been removed - reporting that it could not
             secure the account directories, and leaving every account after
             that one unlocked.  It now says which account it skipped and
             carries on with the rest.
           * A skipped account is left alone deliberately: locking a directory
             to a group that no longer exists would take the account away from
             the person who owns it.

20 Aug 26  The old pre-fork product name is gone from the login code.
           * A banner naming the database SD was forked from was still in
             LOGIN, commented out.  Nothing printed it, but it was one
             character away from appearing at every sign-in.  Removed.
           * NOTHING YOU SEE CHANGES.  The banner you actually get is the
             one below it and is untouched, as is the copyright line.

20 Aug 26  SET.PASSWORD now refuses a password typed on the command line.
           * IT USED TO IGNORE IT SILENTLY.  "SET.PASSWORD DON mypassword"
             set the account to DON, threw the rest away without a word, and
             asked you for the password as though nothing had happened - so
             everything looked like it worked, and you had no way of knowing
             your password had gone into the command stack for nothing.
           * It now stops and says so.  The password is still read from the
             two hidden prompts, exactly as before; nothing else changes.
           * If you have typed one this way, it is worth setting a different
             password, since the old one was recorded where you did not
             intend it to be.

20 Aug 26  The old cleartext API login is gone.  Old clients stop working.
           * THE CHANGE OF 19 AUG ADDED THE NEW LOGIN AND LEFT THE OLD ONE IN
             PLACE.  This removes it.  A client that still sends a password in
             clear is now refused with "Cleartext login is no longer
             supported; this server requires SCRAM authentication".
           * WHAT YOU HAVE TO DO: use a client library from this release or
             later, and run SET.PASSWORD again for every account that uses the
             API.  An account whose password has not been re-set is refused,
             and the refusal reads as a wrong password because from the
             server's point of view there is no credential to check.
           * PROGRAMS THAT USE THE SDCLIENT CLASS ARE COVERED.  The !sdclient
             class module that BASIC programs use to reach another SD server
             now speaks the new login too.  Your code does not change -
             connect() takes the same arguments - but the program has to be
             running under this release at both ends.
           * SDLocal connections are unaffected: they send no password and
             never did.

19 Aug 26  API logins no longer send your password over the connection.
           * The API used to send the user name and password as plain text.
             Anything able to watch the connection could read the password.
             It now uses SCRAM-SHA-256, a challenge and response: the server
             sets a puzzle only someone who knows the password can answer, and
             the password itself is never sent in any form.
           * THE SERVER NOW PROVES ITSELF TO YOU AS WELL.  It finishes by
             returning a value only the real server can compute, and the
             client refuses the connection if it does not match.  Another
             program that grabbed the port before SD started cannot pretend to
             be SD to collect passwords.
           * NOTHING CHANGES IN YOUR APPLICATION.  QMConnect takes the same
             arguments and returns the same things.
           * YOU MUST RUN SET.PASSWORD AGAIN FOR EVERY ACCOUNT THAT USES THE
             API.  The stored credentials changed shape and the old ones
             cannot be converted - the password was never kept anywhere, by
             design, so there is nothing to convert them from.  An account
             whose password has not been re-set will be refused.
           * You also need the new client library.  An older one still works
             for now, but it still sends the password in clear, and a later
             release will stop accepting it.
             [20 Aug 26: that release is the entry above.  An older client is
             refused outright from now on.]

19 Aug 26  OS.EXECUTE now needs permission, as SH already did.
           * UNTIL NOW ANY ACCOUNT THAT COULD WRITE A PROGRAM HAD THE
             OPERATING SYSTEM.  SH at the command prompt was refused unless
             you were listed in OS.USERS or in an elevated session, but
             OS.EXECUTE inside a program was not checked at all - so the
             restriction could be walked around by anyone able to type BASIC.
           * OS.USERS has always had two fields: field 1 is SH and field 2 is
             OS.EX.  Field 2 was written down and never read.  It is now what
             decides whether OS.EXECUTE may run.
           * WHO IS STILL ALLOWED: an account with OS.EX set to "yes"; an
             elevated session, exactly as with SH; and SD's own system
             programs, so CREATE.ACCOUNT and the SH verb work as before.
           * NO FILE AND NO RECORD BOTH MEAN NO, so an installation that has
             never set OS.USERS up denies OS.EXECUTE to ordinary accounts.
             If you have programs that shell out, add the accounts that run
             them to OS.USERS with OS.EX set to yes.
           * The refusal reads: <name> is not permitted to use OS.EXECUTE.

19 Aug 26  The $COMMAND.STACK VOC record is now $command.stack.
           * This is the record that decides whether your command stack is
             saved when you leave SD and restored when you sign in.  It is the
             last VOC name that was still upper case.
           * NOTHING CHANGES FOR YOU.  SD looks for the new name first and the
             old one second, so an account made before this release keeps its
             command stack working with no action from you, and a new one uses
             the new name.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT writes the new name from this release.

19 Aug 26  The system file names in your VOC are lower case.
           * VOC, NEWVOC, ACCOUNTS, MESSAGES, SYSCOM, QFILE, DICT.DICT, MD,
             SD.ACCOUNTS and OS.USERS are now voc, newvoc, accounts, messages,
             syscom, qfile, dict.dict, md, sd.accounts and os.users.  LISTF and
             LIST VOC show the new spelling.
           * YOU DO NOT HAVE TO CHANGE ANYTHING.  Typing a name in either case
             still finds the file, in a command and in a program, so existing
             programs and paragraphs keep working.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP THE OLD SPELLING until their VOC is
             updated - answer Y to "Update VOC to new release?" or run
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  Both spellings work either way.
           * DELETE.FILE still refuses to delete your VOC, whichever case you
             type.

19 Aug 26  Backspace works inside the full-screen editors, and the arrow keys
           no longer type themselves into your record.
           * IN SED AND THE UPDATE.RECORD SCREENS, Backspace deleted the
             character to the RIGHT of the cursor instead of the one to the
             left.  Those editors read the keyboard directly and carry their
             own key tables, so the fix that repaired Backspace at the command
             prompt did not reach them.
           * WHY.  The table said the DEL byte means "Delete".  On a Windows
             console DEL is what the Backspace key sends, and the Delete key
             sends something else entirely.  Backspace now means Backspace, and
             the Delete key has its own entry, so both work and neither has
             taken the other's job.
           * WORSE, IN UPDATE.RECORD THE ARROW KEYS WERE NOT RECOGNISED AT ALL,
             and an unrecognised key is not ignored - its characters were
             entered into the field.  Pressing the right arrow put a "C" in
             your data.  All four arrows now work, as do Home, End, Page Up,
             Page Down, Insert and Delete.
           * ED is unaffected and always was: it is the line editor and reads
             whole lines, so it already had a working Backspace.
           * SED reads its key assignments from a file, so a site that wants
             the old behaviour back can still set it.

19 Aug 26  The arrow keys work, and the default terminal type is now WINDOWS.
           * LEFT, RIGHT, UP and DOWN DID NOTHING in cmd, PowerShell or
             Windows Terminal.  The key was discarded and the cursor stayed
             where it was.
           * WHY.  A terminal has two different ways of spelling an arrow key.
             In its ordinary state it sends ESC [ D for Left; it sends the
             other spelling, ESC O D, only after the application has asked it
             to switch, and SD never asks.  The vt100 definition SD was
             defaulting to since 18 Aug lists only the second spelling, so the
             key SD was listening for was one no Windows console ever sends.
           * The LINUX definition has always had this right - it lists the
             ordinary spelling, and DEL for Backspace, which is exactly what
             every Windows console sends.  Its name describes an operating
             system, but what matters is the byte protocol.
           * SD now ships a WINDOWS terminal definition, an exact copy of
             LINUX, and the LOGIN paragraph in every account's VOC selects it.
             TERM on its own should now say
                 Device    : windows
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP THEIR OLD SETTING until their VOC is
             updated, which happens when you answer Y to "Update VOC to new
             release?" or run UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  Until then, TERM WINDOWS sets
             it for the session.
           * You can still choose your own - TERM WYSE60, say.  All 62
             definitions are still shipped.

19 Aug 26  The terminal definition compiler no longer fails silently.
           * SDTIC compiles terminfo.src into the definitions SD reads.  If one
             definition in the file was malformed it could stop part way
             through, leaving some terminal types missing, and still report
             success - so the first sign of trouble was a terminal that did not
             work.
           * It now compiles every other definition correctly, names the bad
             one with its line number, and returns an error.

19 Aug 26  The Backspace key works.
           * IT DID NOTHING AT ALL in cmd, PowerShell or Windows Terminal, and
             in PuTTY unless you had changed its "Backspace key" setting to
             Control-H.  Typing simply carried on and the character stayed.
           * WHY.  A terminal sends one of two bytes for Backspace - Control-H
             or DEL - and SD accepted only whichever one the terminal type said
             it would.  Every Windows console sends DEL; the terminal type SD
             assumes when TERM is not set is vt100, which says Control-H.  So
             the two never met.
           * Choosing a different terminal type could not have helped: of the
             62 definitions SD ships, 51 say Control-H and only xterm and linux
             say DEL.  That is why vt100-w made no difference.
           * SD now accepts BOTH bytes as Backspace whatever the terminal type,
             so it works out of the box and you need change nothing.  A
             terminal type that deliberately uses DEL for its Delete key - the
             AccuTerm vt100-at definition does - still gets Delete.
           * NOT FIXED YET, and it is the same family: inside the full-screen
             editors (ED and the UPDATE.RECORD screens) Backspace on a terminal
             that sends DEL still deletes forwards.  Those read the keyboard
             directly and carry their own key tables.

19 Aug 26  BASIC no longer creates an object file it can never open again.
           * COMPILING WITH A LOWER-CASE FILE NAME USED TO POISON THE ACCOUNT.
             "BASIC bp MYPROG" built the object file name from what you typed,
             giving bp.OUT, while the directory on disk was made as BP.OUT.  SD
             matches a name as typed, then lower, then upper - never a mixture
             - so nothing could reach bp.OUT afterwards, and the next
             "BASIC BP MYPROG" stopped with "Data pathname 'BP.OUT' already
             exists".  Permanently: nothing cleared it but deleting the file.
           * BASIC now names the object file after the VOC entry that actually
             answered, and the .out suffix follows its case.  A file called bp
             gets bp.out; a file called BP still gets BP.OUT, so nothing that
             works today changes.
           * IF YOU ALREADY HAVE ONE, delete it: DELETE.FILE bp.OUT FORCE, then
             compile again.  FORCE is needed because DELETE.FILE asks about the
             data and dictionary parts separately.

19 Aug 26  The BP and GPL.BP entries in your VOC are now bp and gpl.bp.
           * They join $hold, $savedlists and the commands.  BP.OUT and
             GPL.BP.OUT moved with them.  As always, what you type does not
             change: BASIC BP MYPROG, RUN BP MYPROG and LIST BP all work.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP THEIR OWN SPELLING and go on working.  An
             account created now gets bp.
           * MICRO's "Compile?" prompt is back for lower-case file names.  It
             decided by comparing the last two characters of the file name
             against "BP", which stopped matching once the file was called bp,
             so the offer silently disappeared.  It compares without regard to
             case now.
           * Still upper case, and next: VOC, NEWVOC, ACCOUNTS, MESSAGES,
             SYSCOM, QFILE and $COMMAND.STACK.

19 Aug 26  The system files are now lower case on disk.
           * SD's own files inside C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys are named gpl.bp,
             newvoc, voc_template, messages, syscom, sd.voclib, accounts, bp,
             voc, voc.dic, dict.dic, $hold, $map, $ipc, $cred, os.users and so
             on, instead of GPL.BP, NEWVOC and the rest.  So is the voc file
             inside each account directory.
           * WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE.  These are names on disk, not names
             in your VOC: LISTF, CT VOC and OS_CWD show them, and that is the
             point of the change, but you still type LIST VOC, BASIC BP PROG
             and COUNT ACCOUNTS exactly as before.  The VOC entries themselves
             are renamed separately, one at a time.
           * EXISTING SYSTEMS AND ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED, and there is nothing
             to migrate.  Windows matches file names without regard to case, and
             every one of these files is reached through a name recorded in a
             VOC entry, so a system or an account created before this goes on
             working under its old spelling.  Only newly created ones get the
             new names.
           * The private catalogue (cat), the global catalogue (gcat) and the
             print queue (prt) were already lower case and are unchanged.

18 Aug 26  The commands are now spelled in lower case.
           * LIST, COUNT, SELECT, CREATE.FILE, SETPTR and the rest are stored
             in the VOC as list, count, select, create.file, setptr and so on,
             and that is how they appear in LIST VOC, LISTV and CT VOC.
           * WHAT YOU TYPE DOES NOT CHANGE.  SD tries a name as you typed it,
             then in lower case, then in upper case, so LIST, list and List all
             run the same verb.  Every command you already know still works,
             typed the way you already type it, and so does every keyword -
             WITH, BY, NO.PAGE and the rest.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED.  An account created before this
             keeps the upper-case names in its VOC and goes on working.  There
             is nothing to migrate.  An account you create now, or one you
             refresh with UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, gets the new spelling.
           * The account tiers are unaffected: a standard account is still
             refused the same 18 capabilities, and an administrator still gets
             the same 10 administration verbs.
           * NOT RENAMED, and both on purpose: the file names in your VOC - VOC,
             BP, NEWVOC, GPL.BP, ACCOUNTS, MESSAGES, SYSCOM, QFILE - which move
             with the file-naming change rather than with the commands; and the
             $ records, which are being renamed one at a time.

18 Aug 26  LIST now finds a record whatever case you type its name in.
           * CORRECTION to the entry below, which says the old spelling still
             works when you type it.  That was true of COUNT, CT and ED and NOT
             true of LIST: LIST VOC $HOLD answered "'$HOLD' not found" on the
             very record CT VOC $HOLD showed you.  The two verbs disagreed
             about the same name.
           * LIST, SORT, SELECT and the rest of the query language now try a
             record name as you typed it, then in lower case, then in upper
             case - the same order everything else uses.  A name that matches
             exactly still wins, so nothing that works today changes.
           * A name that exists in NO case is still reported as not found.

18 Aug 26  Names are matched the same way in several more places.
           * SD tries a name as typed, then lower case, then upper case.  A few
             places had been missed and still demanded upper case, so they
             would have stopped working as SD's own names move to lower case.
           * UPDATE.RECORD now recognises its keywords whatever case they are
             stored in; the query processor does the same for VOC entries; the
             LOGIN paragraph is still found by an interactive session and by an
             API session; and SET.FILE still finds the default QFILE pointer.
           * DELETE.FILE's refusal to delete VOC and $ACC no longer depends on
             the case you type.  It could not be got round before, because
             those names are upper case; it could have been once they are not.
           * NOTHING CHANGES ON AN EXISTING SYSTEM.  Every name SD ships is
             still upper case, so the added attempts find nothing that the
             existing ones did not already find.

18 Aug 26  The hold file is now called $hold.
           * SETPTR, SP.VIEW, CLEAN.ACCOUNT, PRINTER FILE and the MICRO editor
             all name it, and the messages they print name it, so $hold is the
             spelling you will see from now on.  SETPTR with no file of its own
             now reports "Hold file: $hold P1" rather than "$HOLD P1".
           * THE OLD SPELLING STILL WORKS WHEN YOU TYPE IT.  COUNT $HOLD,
             CT VOC $HOLD and ED $HOLD recname all still reach the file,
             because SD tries a name as typed, then in lower case, then in
             upper case.  What you will notice is that SD answers in the new
             spelling: type CT VOC $HOLD and the heading says VOC $hold.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED and do not need to be.  An
             account created before this keeps $HOLD in its VOC and goes on
             working - the same fold reaches it upwards.  Nothing to migrate.
           * BP and $COMMAND.STACK have NOT moved.  This is still one file at
             a time, not a sweep.

18 Aug 26  A silent install no longer stops and waits for someone to click.
           * Installing with /VERYSILENT put one message box on screen - the
             one saying SD will not touch an OpenSSH Server you already have -
             and waited there.  Nothing was copied until somebody clicked OK,
             so an unattended install appeared to hang.
           * The box is now skipped in silent mode, along with the others.  It
             also no longer explains why two options are "absent from this
             page" to somebody who was never shown a page.
           * ONLY SILENT INSTALLS ARE AFFECTED.  A normal install shows the
             same messages it always did.

18 Aug 26  The saved select list file is now called $savedlists.
           * SAVE.LIST, GET.LIST, DELETE.LIST, MERGE.LIST, COPY.LIST,
             SAVE.STACK, CLEAN.ACCOUNT and EDIT.LIST all name it, and the
             messages they print name it, so the new spelling is what you will
             see from now on.
           * THE OLD SPELLING STILL WORKS WHEN YOU TYPE IT, and nothing you
             type has to change.  ED $SAVEDLISTS, COUNT $SAVEDLISTS,
             CT VOC $SAVEDLISTS and COPY.LIST ... FROM $SAVEDLISTS all still
             reach the file, because SD tries a name as typed, then in lower
             case, then in upper case - for record ids as well as file names.
             What you will notice is that SD answers in the new spelling: type
             CT VOC $SAVEDLISTS and the heading it prints back says
             VOC $savedlists.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED and do not need to be.  An
             account created before this keeps $SAVEDLISTS in its VOC and goes
             on working unchanged - the upward half of that same fold is what
             reaches it.  There is nothing to migrate.
           * $HOLD, BP and $COMMAND.STACK have NOT moved.  This is one file at
             a time, not a sweep.

18 Aug 26  Programs can now find a file whatever case its name is stored in.
           * A companion to the change further down this file, which fixed the
             same thing for names you TYPE.  It turns out there are two
             separate lookups, and only one of them had been fixed.
           * Commands typed at the colon prompt found a lower-case file.  An
             SDBasic program's OPEN statement did not, because it goes through
             a different route that still matched the name exactly.
           * So a program containing OPEN "$SAVEDLISTS" would have stopped
             working the moment that file was renamed to lower case.  The order
             for OPEN is now the same as everywhere else: as typed, then lower
             case, then upper case.
           * Q-pointers are included: one naming a file that has since been
             renamed still resolves.
           * NOTHING CHANGES ON AN EXISTING SYSTEM, for the same reason as
             before - every name SD ships is upper case, so the new attempt
             finds nothing until lower-case names appear.

18 Aug 26  New accounts get their files with lower-case names on disk.
           * An account you create now holds $hold, $hold.dic, $svlists and bp
             instead of $HOLD, $HOLD.DIC, $SVLISTS and BP.  LISTF and CT VOC
             show the new spelling.
           * The names you TYPE are unchanged - $HOLD, $SAVEDLISTS and BP still
             work, and are still what appears in the VOC.  Only the directory
             name and the path stored against it have moved.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED and do not need to be.  Each
             account's VOC names its own files, and Windows matches file names
             without regard to case, so both spellings keep working.  There is
             nothing to migrate.
           * This is the first step of a longer change towards lower case
             throughout.

18 Aug 26  CORRECTION to the UNLOCK entry further down, which is wrong.
           * That entry says UNLOCK had never worked, because its VOC entry
             carried the text "Verb to unlock records" in the field where the
             record TYPE belongs.  The record was indeed written that way.  The
             conclusion drawn from it was not right.
           * SD ALLOWS THAT ON PURPOSE.  A type code may be followed by comment
             text with no space in between - the same rule PI, PI/open and
             UniVerse follow - and SD tests only the characters belonging to
             the code it is looking for.  So "Verb to unlock records" reads as
             type V with a comment after it, and UNLOCK dispatched normally.
           * So UNLOCK was not repaired, and if you have been restarting SD to
             clear a stuck record lock, UNLOCK would have done it.  Sorry.
           * COPYP carried the same style of entry and was likewise working;
             it was checked before being changed.
           * BOTH RECORDS HAVE STILL BEEN CHANGED to a bare V, because every
             other verb in the file is written that way, and it was the
             inconsistency that produced the wrong conclusion.  Neither verb
             behaves differently.

18 Aug 26  SDNet, the remote file access system, has been removed.
           * SD could open a file held on another SD server by putting
             "server;file" in a VOC entry.  That is gone.  A VOC entry
             containing a semicolon is now simply a file name that does not
             resolve.
           * WHY.  Each server's user name and password were kept in sd.conf,
             obscured with a simple letter substitution that is not encryption,
             and the session ran over port 4245.  There was also no way to
             switch the feature off: the NETFILES setting was read at startup
             and then never consulted by anything.
           * THE API IS NOT AFFECTED.  SDClient and the remote API are a
             separate mechanism, they are unchanged, and they stay protected by
             requiring an ssh tunnel.
           * SET.SERVER, DELETE.SERVER and LIST.SERVERS have gone with it.  Two
             of those three had never worked in any case - their VOC entries
             were malformed.
           * NETFILES is still ACCEPTED in sd.conf, so an existing
             configuration file will not stop SD starting.  It now does
             nothing, which is also what it did before.

18 Aug 26  UNLOCK works.  It never has, on any release this port was built
           from.
           * Its VOC entry carried the description text, "Verb to unlock
             records", in the field where the record TYPE belongs.  SD reads
             that field to decide what the entry is, so UNLOCK was not a verb
             as far as SD was concerned.
           * UNLOCK is what you use to clear a record lock left behind by a
             session that died while holding one.  Until now the only way to
             release such a lock was to stop and restart SD, which disconnects
             everybody.
           * It is an administrator verb; standard and programmer accounts do
             not get it.

18 Aug 26  A file or VOC entry whose name is not upper case can now be found
           whatever case you type it in.
           * SD looked for the name exactly as you typed it and then, if that
             failed, for the UPPER-case version - and nothing else.  So a name
             that is stored in lower case could only ever be reached by typing
             it exactly right.
           * CREATE.FILE testlc is the case that shows it: the file is
             registered as "testlc", the message says "Created DATA part as
             TESTLC", and COUNT TESTLC then answered "File not found" while
             COUNT testlc worked.
           * The order is now: as typed, then lower case, then upper case.
           * NOTHING CHANGES ON AN EXISTING SYSTEM.  Every name SD ships is
             upper case, so the new attempt never finds anything until
             lower-case names start to appear.  This is groundwork.
           * Account names are deliberately NOT included - they are still
             matched in upper case, which is what makes signing in
             case-insensitive.

18 Aug 26  Adding to or removing from the system-wide catalogue now requires
           administrator rights, whichever way you ask for it.
           * CATALOG already required them for the spelled-out form,
             "CATALOG BP MYPROG GLOBAL".  It did NOT require them when the same
             thing was asked for by putting a *, !, _ or $ in front of the
             name - "CATALOG BP $MYPROG" - which is the form most people use.
           * DELETE.CATALOG required nothing at all, by either route.
           * This matters because the system-wide catalogue holds the programs
             SD runs for everybody, $LOGIN among them.  Replacing one ran your
             code in every session on the machine, administrators included;
             deleting one stopped everybody signing in.
           * NOTHING CHANGES FOR LOCAL AND PRIVATE CATALOGUING, which is what
             programmers use day to day:
                 CATALOG BP MYPROG          private catalogue, this account
                 CATALOG BP MYPROG LOCAL    this account's VOC
             Both still work, in any account you are allowed to LOGTO into.
           * The only thing you can no longer do as an ordinary user is
             catalogue a program whose name starts with *, !, _ or $, because
             those characters mean "system-wide".  Name it without one.
           * The system-wide catalogue is now locked at the Windows level as
             well, not only inside SD: SD users may run what is in it and
             nothing else.  So a program you did not install cannot replace
             one that everybody runs, whichever way it is reached.
           * TO CATALOGUE SYSTEM-WIDE YOU NOW NEED AN ELEVATED SESSION, not
             just an administrator account.  Start SD from an elevated window
             and LOGTO SDSYS.  Entering SDSYS from an ordinary window still
             gives you administrator rights inside SD, but Windows itself now
             refuses the write - so use the same elevated window you already
             need in order to recompile the system programs.

18 Aug 26  The default terminal type is now VT100, not LINUX.
           * Every account's VOC holds a LOGIN paragraph that sets the
             terminal type when you sign in.  It said LINUX, inherited from
             the Linux original; on Windows the sensible default is VT100.
           * TERM on its own reports what you are using.  It should now say
                 Device    : vt100
           * You can still choose your own - "TERM WYSE60", say - and you can
             edit the LOGIN paragraph in your VOC if you want a different
             default for one account.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS KEEP LINUX until their VOC is updated, which
             happens when you answer Y to "Update VOC to new release?" or run
             UPDATE.ACCOUNTS.  The release number changed in this same version,
             so you will be asked anyway on your next sign-in.

18 Aug 26  The release is W1.0-0.  The login header now reads "version W1.0-0".
           * The W is for Windows.  1.0-1 and 1.0-2 were releases of SD this
             port was built from, not releases of the port itself.
           * IF YOU ALREADY HAVE AN OLDER TREE INSTALLED, signing in will ask
             "Your VOC is at release level 1.0-2 / Update VOC to new release?"
             Answer Y.  It compares the two for difference, not for which is
             newer.
           * The installer is now sd-setup-W1.0-0.exe.
           * Nothing else moved.  The QM revision SD reports for file and
             compatibility purposes is still 2.6-6.

18 Aug 26  Who may use SH is now a list you keep, not a matter of elevation.
           * SH used to need an elevated session, and an ssh session can never
             be elevated - so programmers, the people who actually need a
             shell, were the ones who could never have one.
           * Name them instead.  From SDSYS:
                 ED OS.USERS don
             one record per person, keyed by the name they sign in with, NOT
             the account name.  Field 1 is SH and field 2 is OS.EX; put "yes"
             in field 1 to give that person a shell.  A missing record, or a
             missing file, means no.
           * The name is the LOGIN name.  It follows the person, so it does
             not change when they LOGTO somewhere else.
           * ONLY AN ADMINISTRATOR CAN EDIT THE LIST.  It is read-only to
             everybody else on disk, which is the whole of what stops a user
             granting themselves a shell.  Do not loosen those permissions.
           * A LISTED PERSON GETS A REAL SHELL - pipes, redirection and
             chaining all work:
                 SH dir | more
             Anyone not on the list who reaches SH by being elevated still
             gets the restricted form, which rejects those characters.
           * An elevated session keeps SH whatever the list says, deliberately,
             so an empty list cannot lock the machine's own administrator out.
           * FIELD 2, OS.EX, IS STORED BUT NOT YET ENFORCED.  It is there for
             a later release that will gate OS.EXECUTE from inside a program.
             Until then, anyone who can write and run SDBasic can still reach
             the operating system that way, whatever field 2 says.

17 Aug 26  Signing in over ssh now takes you straight into SD.
           * An ssh session lands in SD instead of at a Windows prompt.  That
             applies to everyone who may connect, administrators included:
             the rule is about the route in, not about who took it.
           * SCP AND SFTP NO LONGER WORK INTO THIS MACHINE, for anybody.  The
             command is forced, so there is no file-transfer subsystem left
             to run.  This is the cost of the above and is deliberate.
           * TO COPY FILES, PULL THEM RATHER THAN PUSHING.  Sit at the machine
             or connect with Remote Desktop, and use WinSCP or scp from there
             to fetch what you need.  Outbound connections are not affected -
             only sessions coming in.
           * Remote Desktop and the local console are untouched, and are how
             an administrator reaches the machine itself.
           * IT IS NOT APPLIED BY THE INSTALLER on a machine that already had
             an ssh server.  Run this once from an elevated prompt:
                 powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\allow-ssh-groups.ps1" -Installed
             It checks the result with "sshd -T" and puts the original back if
             anything is wrong, keeping a copy as sshd_config.before-sd.

17 Aug 26  Queries against a directory file now match ids case insensitively.
           * SELECT and LIST against a DIRECTORY file now match a record id
             whatever case you type it in, which is how Windows treats the
             file names those ids are:
                 SELECT BP WITH @ID = "sue"      finds record SUE
           * This never worked before.  SD asks itself whether it is running
             on Windows, and on this port it was answering NO, so the code
             that does case insensitive matching was never reached.
           * DYNAMIC FILES ARE UNCHANGED and stay case sensitive unless the
             file itself is marked otherwise, so nothing you already rely on
             changes:
                 SELECT VOC WITH @ID = "who"     still finds nothing
           * SYSTEM(91) now answers 1 rather than 0.  If you have written a
             program that asks it, it will start telling the truth.

17 Aug 26  Record ids in directory files are no longer case sensitive.
           * Windows treats SUE and sue as the same file, but SD did not, so
             for a DIRECTORY file it could believe there were two records
             where the disk held one.  It now agrees with Windows.
           * WHAT YOU WILL NOTICE is locking.  A program holding a record
             lock on 'sue' now correctly blocks another one asking for
             'SUE'; before, both could hold what they thought were separate
             locks on the same file.  Transactions match ids the same way.
           * FILEINFO(f, FL$NOCASE) answers 1 for a directory file, where it
             used to answer 0.
           * DYNAMIC FILES ARE NOT AFFECTED.  They keep the setting recorded
             in their own file header, exactly as before, so nothing about an
             existing file changes and there is nothing to convert.
           * Nothing is stored differently and no file needs rebuilding.

17 Aug 26  SECURITY: stored passwords are protected from other SD users.
           * On installs made before this, every SD user could WRITE to the
             file SD keeps account passwords in.  No password is stored there
             - SD keeps a scrambled verifier, and that cannot be turned back
             into a password - but being able to REPLACE one was enough: a
             user could put in a verifier for a password of their own
             choosing and then sign in as somebody else, including through
             the API.
           * The installer was always meant to lock that file down and never
             did.  The step ran, reported nothing, and had no effect, because
             of a quoting fault in how it was called.  Nothing in the install
             looked wrong.
           * IF YOU HAVE ALREADY INSTALLED SD, REINSTALLING IS WHAT FIXES IT.
             You can check without reinstalling: from an ordinary (not
             administrator) PowerShell prompt, run
                 icacls "C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\$CRED"
             If that prints permissions, the file is still open.  If it says
             "Access is denied", it is protected, which is what you want.
           * Nothing you use day to day changes.  SET.PASSWORD still works
             for administrators, and API sign-in is unaffected.

17 Aug 26  SET.PASSWORD can be reached at last, and administrators have it.
           * An SD account can now be given a password.  The command existed
             and no VOC pointed at it, so there was no way to type it.
           * SET.PASSWORD <account> sets somebody else's and needs
             administrator rights.  SET.PASSWORD on its own sets your own, and
             asks for your current one first.
           * IT IS AN ADMINISTRATOR COMMAND, so only ADMINISTRATOR accounts
             have it to begin with.  If you want your users setting their own
             passwords, copy SET.PASSWORD into their VOC - the command already
             knows the difference and will still ask them for the current one.
           * You need this before the API will let anybody in: APILOGIN=1
             means an account with no password cannot be reached at all.

17 Aug 26  A program on another computer can reach SD again, over ssh.
           * SD can now listen for API (SDClient) connections.  It is OFF
             until you turn it on: put APIPORT=4243 in sd.conf and restart SD.
             Nothing is opened on a system that does not ask for it.
           * The port is bound to this machine only.  Reach it from elsewhere
             by forwarding it over ssh, which is how SD is meant to be used
             from off the machine:
                 ssh -L 4243:127.0.0.1:4243 <user>@<host>
             then point your client at 127.0.0.1 port 4243 on your own end.
           * Your program still signs in with an account name and password,
             and can only enter accounts it has been granted, exactly as a
             person at a terminal can.  Opening the port does not hand
             anybody your data.
           * ON LINUX THIS JOB WAS DONE BY xinetd OR systemd.  Windows has
             neither, so SD now does it itself; you do not install or
             configure anything else.
           * SAY CONFIG('APIPORT') to see the port SD is listening on.
           * Note for anyone moving a configuration file from a Linux
             installation: the Linux build reaches SD through a socket file
             under /tmp, which does not exist here and cannot be forwarded on
             Windows.  APIPORT replaces it.

17 Aug 26  Connecting a program to SD on the same machine now actually works.
           * SDConnectLocal() in the client library opens a session on this
             computer with no network and no password: SD takes your identity
             from the account your program is running as.
           * It was reported as built once before and did not work.  It does
             now - a session opens, commands run, and an account you have not
             been granted is refused.
           * If you wrote anything against it in the last day, no change is
             needed on your side; the fix is inside the library.

17 Aug 26  An SD administrator's account now holds the administration verbs,
           and the tier an account was given is remembered.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR now puts CREATE.ACCOUNT,
             DELETE.ACCOUNT, MODIFY.ACCOUNT, UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, GRANT, REVOKE,
             LIST.GRANTS, UNLOCK and ENCRYPT.FIELD into the account's own VOC.
             Until now they were in SDSYS alone, so an administrator had to
             LOGTO SDSYS before they could administer anything.
           * The verbs still check for themselves that you are an
             administrator, so having them in a VOC is not what permits them.
           * MODIFY, COMPILE.DICT, CD, GENERATE and PHANTOM have joined the
             list a standard account does not get.  They edit records, compile
             dictionaries or start background programs, which is work for a
             programmer.
           * WHICH TIER AN ACCOUNT WAS CREATED AS IS NOW RECORDED, in field 5
             of its record in the ACCOUNTS file.  Say UPDATE.ACCOUNTS, or
             answer Yes when a new release offers to update your VOC, and a
             standard account no longer gets the withheld verbs back.  That
             was a way round the whole thing and needed no privilege.
           * Accounts created before this have nothing in field 5, and are
             treated as they always were - a VOC update gives them every verb.
           * The account of the person who installs SD is created as an
             administrator, so their VOC is complete.

17 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT has a PROGRAMMER keyword, and a plain account is
           now smaller than it was.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> PROGRAMMER gives the account the full
             set of verbs: BASIC, CATALOG, RUN, ED, EDIT, SED, COPY, COPYP,
             DELETE.CATALOG, SH and !.  ADMINISTRATOR includes all of that.
           * WITHOUT EITHER KEYWORD the account no longer gets those verbs.
             It can run an application; it cannot compile, catalogue, edit or
             shell out.  Until now every account got exactly the same verbs
             whichever keyword you used.
           * If somebody needs one of them, copy the verb into their VOC from
             SDSYS - that is a normal COPY and takes a moment.  This is a
             starting posture, not a lock.
           * The list lives in SDSYS NEWVOC as the record TIER.OMIT.STANDARD,
             so you can change what a standard account starts with.
           * EXISTING ACCOUNTS ARE NOT TOUCHED.  This applies to accounts made
             from now on.

17 Aug 26  MICRO and the language verbs are gone.
           * MICRO launched an external editor, which is a way out of SD onto
             the machine underneath it.  It is removed for the same reason SH
             is restricted.  REVERSED ON 26 Aug 26 - see the EDIT entry at the
             top.  A full-screen editor is back, under the name EDIT, limited
             to programmer and administrator accounts.
           * NLS, SET.LANGUAGE and LOAD.LANGUAGE are removed: SD is English
             only, and they were the only callers of the message-language
             machinery.
           * If you had a use for any of them, say so - an administrator can
             put a verb back into an account's VOC, and the programs behind
             these are still installed.

17 Aug 26  Connecting a program to SD on the same machine now works.
           * SDConnectLocal() in the client library opens a connection to SD
             on this computer, with no network and no password: SD identifies
             you from the account you are running as.
           * It could not have worked before, on this or any other version.
             The library started SD with the name of a pipe, and SD only
             understood a pair of file numbers, so it stopped immediately.
             The library also looked for sd.exe inside the database folder
             rather than where it is installed.
           * The client library's own README and user guide said this function
             was not provided on Windows.  They were wrong, and are corrected.

17 Aug 26  The API only lets you into accounts you have been granted.
           * Connecting to an account through the API now applies the same
             rule LOGTO applies inside SD: you may enter an account if you
             are a member of the Windows group SD created for it.  GRANT and
             REVOKE are how that membership is given and taken away.
           * Until now an API session that authenticated could switch to ANY
             account by name.  The check existed before the Windows port and
             was lost in it, because it tested a Linux group.
           * A refusal reads "User not allowed in requested account", which
             is also what you get for an account that does not exist.  That
             is deliberate: it stops the API being used to find out which
             accounts are there.
           * SDSYS is not reachable through the API, by design.  Administra-
             tion needs elevation and an API session cannot have it.

17 Aug 26  The API checks the SD account password, not a Windows one.
           * Set it with SET.PASSWORD, from inside SD.  An account that has
             never had one set cannot be reached through the API at all.
           * This replaces a check against the operating system's own password
             file, which does not exist on Windows - so the API could not
             accept a login on this platform at any point before now.  It
             refused everyone rather than letting anyone in, but it refused
             everyone.
           * The ssh tunnel is still the first gate and is unchanged.  Two
             gates, not one: reaching the machine is not reaching SD.
           * NOT YET USABLE END TO END.  The transport the Linux client uses -
             ssh forwarding a local port to a UNIX socket - cannot work on
             Windows, so this fixes the login and not yet the connection.

16 Aug 26  The CREATUSR configuration parameter is gone.
           * CONFIG no longer lists it, and config('CREATUSR') no longer
             returns a value.  It stopped having any effect on 14 Aug 26,
             when CREATE.ACCOUNT and DELETE.ACCOUNT stopped consulting it:
             SD has accounts, not accounts and users, so creating an account
             creates the operating system account and there is nothing to
             opt in to.
           * A CREATUSR line in sd.conf is still accepted and ignored, so a
             configuration file carrying one - your own, or one brought from
             a Linux install - still works.  You can delete the line.

16 Aug 26  The installer now ALWAYS installs an OpenSSH server, instead of
           offering it as a choice that was off by default.

           The reason is that accounts SD creates sign in over ssh and nothing
           else - they are refused at the console and over Remote Desktop.
           Without an ssh server on the machine those accounts had no way in
           at all, so an install that declined ssh was one that nobody but the
           person who installed it could use.  That is true even on a machine
           with no network and one person at the keyboard: you reach SD by
           connecting with ssh to "localhost", which works with no network of
           any kind.

           IF YOUR MACHINE ALREADY HAS AN SSH SERVER, SD LEAVES IT ALONE.  It
           installs nothing, restarts nothing, and changes neither its
           configuration nor its firewall rule.  That has not changed.

           Installing it is a download from Windows Update.  It can take
           several minutes with nothing on screen, and it usually asks for a
           restart afterwards; until you restart, no SD account except your
           own can sign in.  The installer says so before you start and again
           when it finishes.  If the download is blocked - by company policy,
           or a metered or absent connection - SD still installs and works for
           you, and the closing message tells you plainly that nobody else can
           use it until an ssh server exists.

16 Aug 26  New option: "Let other computers on your network connect to this
           one over ssh".  It is off by default, and off means ssh works only
           from the machine itself.

           This is stricter than what came before.  Installing the OpenSSH
           server makes Windows open port 22 to your local network, so ticking
           the old "install OpenSSH" box quietly did that too.  Now that the
           server is always installed, letting other computers reach it is a
           separate decision that you have to make deliberately.

           To change your mind later, from an elevated PowerShell prompt:

               powershell -File "C:\Program Files\SD\ssh-firewall.ps1" -Installed -Open

           and -Restrict puts it back to this machine only.  Uninstalling SD
           does not reopen the port.

16 Aug 26  The message at the end of the installer is about half as long.  It
           now tells you only what could not be known in advance - what
           happened to the ssh server, to the firewall setting and to your SD
           account - plus the one thing you have to act on, which is signing
           out and back in.  Everything else it used to say is on the new page
           at the start, where you can read it before deciding to install.

16 Aug 26  The installer now begins with a page listing everything it changes
           on your computer - the groups it creates, the sign-in rights it
           denies, the permissions it rewrites on the database, the service it
           installs, the PATH entry, and the OpenSSH install with its minutes
           and its restart.  You can read it and click Cancel before anything
           has happened.

           It is there because most of that was previously only discoverable
           after the fact, and because the ssh question above is one you were
           being asked to answer with no context.

16 Aug 26  The option to limit ssh to SD users and administrators has moved to
           the main options page.  It used to be a sub-option under "install
           OpenSSH", which no longer exists.  Its description now also says
           that it stops scp and sftp working, which it always did.

16 Aug 26  Uninstalling SD no longer leaves an empty entry on the system PATH.
           It had been removing the SD directory but leaving the separator
           behind, so every install-and-uninstall cycle added one more empty
           slot - visible as a run of semicolons in the PATH, which looks
           alarmingly as though SD was never added at all.  The next uninstall
           clears the whole accumulated run, not just the one it creates.

           If SD looks absent from your PATH just after installing, check a
           NEW command prompt first.  Windows tells running programs the PATH
           has changed, but a shell that is already open keeps the copy it
           started with.

16 Aug 26  SECURITY.  When SD does privileged work for you - creating an
           account, setting a password, editing a group - it writes a short
           script to a file and runs it, rather than putting a password on a
           command line where anyone could read it.  Those files were being
           written into the database directory, which every SD user can write
           to.  Two things followed, and the second is the serious one:

             * Another SD user could read a new Windows password out of the
               file in the moment before it was deleted.

             * Another SD user could REPLACE the script between SD writing it
               and SD running it, and their version would run with full
               administrator privilege.

           The scripts now go in a directory of their own, PSTMP inside the
           database directory, where each file belongs to the session that
           wrote it.  Other SD users cannot read those files or change them;
           administrators still can.  The installer creates the directory and
           empties it, so a script left behind by an interrupted session does
           not survive an upgrade.

           If that directory is missing, SD refuses to do the privileged work
           rather than falling back to the old location.  You would see the
           command fail rather than quietly running unprotected.

16 Aug 26  SD now keeps a log of the elevation helper's work, in the file
           "sd-elevate.log" in C:\ProgramData\SD, beside the service log.  It
           records when a helper started for a session, each script it was
           asked to run and the exit code that came back, and when it stopped.

           ONLY ADMINISTRATORS CAN READ OR WRITE IT.  Ordinary SD users are not
           on its permissions at all - they cannot read it, add to it, change
           it or delete it.  That is different from the audit trail, which SD
           users CAN add to because SD writes it as them; nothing unelevated
           ever writes this one.

           It is a diagnostic, not the audit trail.  If you want to know who
           obtained privilege and when, read "audit".  This file is for the
           question "the account was not created - what actually happened",
           which previously had no answer at all.

           If the file is missing, nothing is logged and SD does not create
           one.  That is deliberate: a log created on the fly would inherit
           permissions letting every SD user rewrite it, and a record of
           privileged work that its own subjects can edit is worse than none.
           A reinstall keeps whatever is already there.

16 Aug 26  LIST.GRANTS works again.  It had been answering "Cannot read the
           members of group <name>, status: 5" for every account, whoever
           asked.  Reading a group's membership needs no special privilege and
           never did; the check that was refusing it should not have been on
           that command at all.  GRANT and REVOKE were unaffected.

16 Aug 26  Entering SDSYS no longer needs an elevated window.  Type "sd" at an
           ordinary command prompt, then "LOGTO SDSYS", and Windows asks you to
           confirm - the usual "Do you want to allow this app to make changes?"
           box.  Say yes and you are in SDSYS and can do the work that needs
           privilege, such as CREATE.ACCOUNT.  Say no and the LOGTO is refused.
           Leaving SDSYS with another LOGTO gives the privilege up again.

           This reverses the 14 Aug entry below, which said an elevated window
           was the only route into SDSYS.  That was never the intention.  An
           elevated window makes EVERYTHING you type privileged; this way, only
           what SD does on your behalf is privileged, and only while you are
           in SDSYS.

           There is no ELEVATE command and there will not be one.  Entering
           SDSYS is the only thing that asks Windows for privilege.

           IT STILL DOES NOT WORK OVER ssh, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.  Windows
           draws that confirmation box on the desktop and an ssh session has
           no desktop, so the LOGTO is refused there.  Administering SD means
           being at the machine, or using a remote-control tool that gives you
           a real desktop session - and such a tool must be INSTALLED AS A
           SERVICE, or it cannot show you the box and your screen will appear
           to freeze instead.

           The audit trail records this: "ELEVATION GRANTED" when you consent,
           "ELEVATION RELEASED" when you leave SDSYS, and a refused LOGTO
           giving "elevation refused or unavailable" as the reason when you say
           no or when there is no desktop to ask on.

16 Aug 26  SD now keeps an audit trail, in the file "audit" beside the
           database in C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys.  It records every login,
           every login that was refused, every LOGTO, every LOGTO that was
           refused, and every GRANT and REVOKE, each with the date, the time
           and the Windows user it belonged to.  A line looks like

              2026-08-16 11:42:07 user=don uid=1 pid=8624 LOGTO account=SDSYS

           The refusals are the interesting half.  An entry saying somebody
           who is not an administrator asked for SDSYS by name, or asked for
           an account they have not been granted, is the thing worth seeing.

           This file is NOT the error log and does not behave like it.  The
           error log throws away its oldest half when it fills up; the audit
           file is renamed with the date and time and a new one started, so
           nothing is ever discarded.  Removing the old ones is your decision
           - SD will not do it for you, and they will accumulate.

           SD users can ADD to this file and can do nothing else to it.  They
           cannot read it, cannot change a record already in it, cannot empty
           it, and cannot rename or delete it - Windows itself refuses, not SD.
           So a user cannot quietly remove the line that records what they
           did.  Administrators can read it, and can do anything else to it
           as well: this raises the floor against ordinary users rather than
           trying to constrain somebody who owns the machine.

           Windows keeps its own separate record of account and group changes
           in the Security event log.  Read the two together.

16 Aug 26  SD now starts after a restart even if the last shutdown was not a
           clean one.  If SD is stopped abruptly - the power goes, or the
           process is killed - it leaves a shared memory segment behind, and
           on Windows that survives a reboot where on Linux it would not.
           SD used to refuse to start on the next boot and say "Run sd -stop
           to clear it", which meant the machine came up with SD unavailable
           to everybody until somebody logged in and typed it by hand.
           Nothing from before a restart can still be using that segment, so
           SD now discards it and starts normally, printing

              Discarding the shared segment left by the previous boot -
              SD did not shut down cleanly.

           This changes nothing while the machine is running: a segment that
           belongs to a live SD is still never touched, and "sd -start" still
           refuses to disturb a system that is already up.

15 Aug 26  SD now runs as a Windows service, called "String Database (SD)".
           The installer creates it and starts it, and Windows starts it
           again after every restart - so SD is simply up, and nobody has to
           type "sd -start" any more.  Stopping the service stops SD and ends
           every session on the machine, which is the same thing "sd -stop"
           has always done.  Uninstalling removes the service.

15 Aug 26  You now always log in to your OWN account, and move with LOGTO.
           Before, an elevated session that typed plain "sd" went straight
           into SDSYS, and "sd -Asomebodyelse" let an administrator open
           anyone's account without ever being in their own.  Now everybody
           arrives in the account named after their Windows login, including
           administrators, and reaches other accounts with

              LOGTO <account>

           which is where SD checks whether you are allowed in.  An
           administrator still reaches every account - just afterwards
           rather than instead.  "sd -A<name>" is refused unless <name> is
           your own account.

15 Aug 26  Three new verbs let you share an account with somebody else:

              GRANT <account> TO <user>
              REVOKE <account> FROM <user>
              LIST.GRANTS <account>

           All three need an elevated session, and they say so rather than
           failing obscurely.  There is one thing about them worth reading
           twice, because it is the most confusing part of how SD controls
           access: WINDOWS FIXES GROUP MEMBERSHIP WHEN YOU SIGN IN, so a
           grant does not reach the person until they sign out of Windows
           and back in - and, more importantly, SOMEBODY YOU HAVE JUST
           REVOKED KEEPS THE ACCOUNT UNTIL THEY DO THE SAME.  Both verbs
           print that reminder every time.

           "LIST.GRANTS" also shows the account's own user, which is always
           there and is not a grant.  It is listed anyway so that what you
           see matches the Windows group it is reporting.

           Behind this: entry to an account is membership of the Windows
           group named in the account's record, so these verbs edit that
           group and change nothing inside SD.  The old field 4 of an
           ACCOUNTS record, a list of accounts allowed in, has been removed -
           nothing had consulted it since 14 Aug.  "LIST ACCOUNTS" no longer
           shows a "Granted to" column; LIST.GRANTS answers that now.

15 Aug 26  The installer no longer suggests "net localgroup sdusers <name>
           /add" for giving somebody who already has a Windows account access
           to SD.  It did not work: being in sdusers lets you read SD's files,
           but signing in needs an SD account linked to your Windows one, and
           somebody added to the group and nothing else is turned away with
           "Account X not in register".  Use CREATE.ACCOUNT, which makes both
           halves together.

15 Aug 26  Connecting over ssh puts you straight into SD.  Sessions used to
           arrive at a Windows shell prompt, which an SD account has no use
           for and no business with.  Now every ssh session runs SD instead -
           including an administrator's, because an ssh session cannot be
           elevated in any case and lands in your own account rather than
           SDSYS.  The console, Remote Desktop and remote-control tools are
           NOT affected; this is ssh only.  It does mean NOTHING CAN BE RUN
           OVER ssh WITHOUT A PERSON AT IT: scp and sftp stop working, and so
           does "ssh thismachine somecommand" - the command is discarded and
           SD runs instead, so a script that expects output waits for ever at
           SD's ":" prompt.  Send OFF on standard input if you need such a
           session to end by itself.

15 Aug 26  Anything typed after "sd" now needs an elevated session.  That
           covers the switches you would type at a prompt - -start, -stop,
           -k, -l and -u among them - and it also covers a command:
           "sd LISTF" ran LISTF for anybody, and no longer does.
           Typing plain "sd" to use your own account is unchanged, and that is
           the whole of what an ordinary session needs: accounts SD creates
           arrive in SD over ssh anyway.  NOTE: SD does not start at boot, so
           after a restart somebody with administrator rights has to start it.

15 Aug 26  SD will not start a second time inside itself.  If you leave SD
           with SH and then type "sd" in that shell, it says so and returns
           you to the session you already have.  Worth knowing alongside it:
           SH itself needs an elevated session, so an ordinary account cannot
           leave SD this way at all, and one reached over ssh never can.

15 Aug 26  Installing SD now gives you an SD account.  Until now the person who
           installed SD was the one person who could not use it: every SD
           account brings its own Windows account, and yours already existed,
           so typing "sd" answered that your account was not in the register.
           The install now attaches an SD account to your Windows login, and
           the closing dialog says so.  Nothing to set, and no password: your
           Windows session is what identifies you.

15 Aug 26  An administrator is never confined to ssh.  Accounts SD creates
           normally sign in over ssh only, and that restriction was being
           applied to accounts it merely attached to an existing Windows login
           - including the installer's.  The effect was invisible until the
           next sign-in, and then locked the machine's own administrator out of
           their console and Remote Desktop.  Two rules now hold: an account SD
           only attaches to keeps exactly the Windows sign-in rights it already
           had, and no Windows administrator is ever put in the ssh-only group,
           whatever route created the account.  An administrator created inside
           SD can therefore use both the computer and SD, which is the point of
           creating one.

14 Aug 26  LIST ACCOUNTS says "Windows Group", not "Linux Group".  Existing
           databases keep the old label until they are rebuilt - the wording
           lives in the dictionary, which is built when SD is installed.

14 Aug 26  Accounts: DELETE.ACCOUNT is careful about your Windows accounts, and
           CREATE.ACCOUNT says what it will not do.
           * An SD account brings its own Windows account.  CREATE.ACCOUNT
             creates one, and it now REFUSES a name that already exists in
             Windows instead of failing with "Create User Failed, OS Error: 1",
             which is what it used to say and which read like a fault.  The one
             exception is the account of whoever installs SD, which the install
             attaches to their existing Windows login.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT only offers to remove the Windows account if SD
             created it.  If it was there before SD, it is left alone and the
             verb says so - deleting an SD account can no longer take somebody's
             Windows login with it.  If the Windows account has already gone,
             it says that too, and cleans up the SD side.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT now removes the account's sdu_ group, which it
             never actually did.  It asked Windows a question in a Linux way
             and was told the group did not exist, every time.
           * The same mistake made SD unable to tell whether a Windows user
             existed.  Both are fixed.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT removes the register entry last.  If something
             fails part way through, the account is still registered and you
             can simply run the verb again.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER x NO.QUERY now explains itself: creating a
             Windows account means setting its password, and that needs a
             prompt, so NO.QUERY cannot work for a user account.

14 Aug 26  "sd -start" and "sd -stop" no longer claim things about the sdwind
           daemon that are not true.  Both used to answer from the shared
           memory segment, which outlives the daemon, so both could report
           success while doing nothing.
           * "SD is already started" is now only said when the daemon really
             is running, and it tells you the process id - the Windows one,
             the number Task Manager and Stop-Process use.
           * If the segment is there but the daemon is not, which is what a
             killed or crashed SD leaves behind, "sd -start" says so and tells
             you to run "sd -stop" first.  It used to say "SD is already
             started" and do nothing, leaving the system unusable while the
             command that fixes it reported success.  It does not clear the
             wreckage for you: that would end any sessions still attached to
             it, and the count of those is printed so you can decide.
           * "sd -stop" now checks that the daemon actually stopped.  One
             started from an elevated session cannot be stopped from an
             ordinary one - Windows refuses the signal - and that used to be
             silent, leaving a daemon running against a segment nothing else
             could see.  You now get a warning naming the process id and the
             command to stop it with.
           * Known limit: if the segment has already gone, "sd -stop" has
             nowhere left to read the daemon's process id from and cannot
             report on it at all.  Check with "Get-Process sdwind".

14 Aug 26  The sign-on banner now says "SD for Windows" and no longer carries
           the "(AI modified)" note.

14 Aug 26  Signing in asks for nothing, and administration belongs to Windows.
           This reverses the 13 Aug identity entry further down.  Built,
           installed and VERIFIED END TO END: an ordinary account signs in over
           ssh and lands in its own account with nothing asked, an unelevated
           session is refused at both doors, LOGTO into SDSYS and into somebody
           else's account are both refused, and an elevated session reaches
           SDSYS.  See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 4 for what was observed.
           * Type "sd" and you are in your own account.  Windows has already
             signed you in, so SD asks it who you are and takes no password.
             The account is the one named after your Windows user name.
           * No SD account of that name means no login.  You are refused at the
             door rather than asked to name another account.
           * You must be in the sdusers group to use SD at all.
           * Run SD elevated - "as administrator" - and you land straight in
             SDSYS with nothing typed.  That is the only route into SDSYS.
             "sd -ASDSYS" and LOGTO SDSYS are both refused to a session that is
             not elevated.
           * Being an administrator is not enough by itself; you have to have
             used it.  An administrator who has not elevated is an ordinary SD
             user, which is what "sudo" means on Linux.
           * ELEVATION IS DELIBERATELY LOCAL.  Windows does not offer it over
             ssh, so SDSYS is reached from the machine itself or from a remote
             client that gives you a real desktop session.  Administrators
             having less remote access than ordinary users is the intent, not
             an oversight.
           * LOGTO into another account is allowed if you are a member of that
             account's group - the sdu_ group CREATE.ACCOUNT makes for it - so
             granting somebody a second account means adding them to that
             group.
           * GRANTS ARE GROUP MEMBERSHIP, and the "Granted to" list is on its
             way out.  ACCOUNTS field 4 is still there and LIST ACCOUNTS still
             shows it, but nothing reads it any more, so any grant recorded on
             13 or 14 Aug has stopped having an effect.  Give somebody an
             account by adding them to its sdu_ group instead.  The column will
             be removed, and GRANT and REVOKE verbs will do the group edit for
             you - neither exists yet, so for now it is Windows' own group
             tools, from an elevated session.
           * SOMEBODY YOU GRANT AN ACCOUNT TO MUST SIGN OUT AND BACK IN BEFORE
             THEY CAN USE IT.  Windows fixes your group membership when you sign
             in, so a session that was already open cannot see the change, and
             being elevated does not help - the elevated token comes from the
             same sign-in.  Until they sign back in, SD refuses them with "User
             not allowed in requested account", which looks exactly like the
             grant not having worked.
           * LOGTO SDSYS no longer asks for your password a second time.  The
             gate is applied when you sign in, so there is nothing to step up
             into, and messages 10030 and 10031 are no longer used.
           * Account passwords are NOT gone.  SET.PASSWORD, the $CRED register
             and the stored verifiers all remain, and become the API's
             credential rather than the console's.  Nothing on the console asks
             for one today.

14 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT is confirmed working, ssh restriction and all.
           * An account created with CREATE.ACCOUNT USER was signed in over
             ssh with the password SD set for it, and refused a local sign-in
             at the same moment.  So the account you get from the verb really
             is the ssh-only account it says it is.
           * Everything else it makes was checked too: the Windows account is
             created and enabled, it joins sdusers and sdsshonly and its own
             sdu_ group, it is NOT an administrator unless you say
             ADMINISTRATOR, and the account directory, VOC, BP and the
             register entry are all there.
           * You will see "Warning unable to setgid bit on Group Folder,
             status: 1" every time.  It is harmless and can be ignored - it is
             a leftover of the Linux version and does nothing on Windows.
           * Remote Desktop is still the one part nobody has watched refuse.

14 Aug 26  Reinstalling does not update your database, and it never did.
           * Worth knowing before you report a bug that is already fixed.  The
             installer deliberately leaves an existing database alone, so
             installing a newer SD over an older one replaces the programs but
             NOT the system files under C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys - which is
             where SD's own commands live.
           * So a fix to a command can be installed and still not be present.
             On 14 Aug 26 that cost a full investigation of a CREATE.ACCOUNT
             failure that had been fixed an hour earlier.
           * There is no upgrade path yet.  To get a genuinely current system,
             uninstall, delete C:\ProgramData\SD, and install again - which
             deletes your accounts and data, so it is only reasonable while
             there is nothing in there you want.

14 Aug 26  The installer can also limit who may ssh in at all.
           * A new tick box, under the OpenSSH one: "Also limit ssh to SD users
             and administrators".  It writes an AllowGroups line into
             sshd_config naming "sdusers" and your administrators group, so
             nobody else on the machine can ssh in even if they have a Windows
             account.  Off by default.
           * It is only offered when SD is installing OpenSSH itself.  If you
             already had an ssh server, SD does not touch its configuration -
             it may be there for something else.
           * Your original sshd_config is kept as sshd_config.before-sd, and
             uninstalling SD takes the line back out again.
           * If OpenSSH has just been installed and asked you to restart, there
             is no configuration file to edit yet and the installer will say
             so, with the one command to run after restarting.
           * CONFIRMED BY REAL SSH CONNECTIONS on 14 Aug 26.  An account in
             the allowed groups still got in; an account in neither was
             refused, with the reason written to the ssh server's log.  The
             machine's own administrator kept ssh throughout, which was the
             risk worth checking.
           * If you turn this on, be aware the person refused sees the same
             "Permission denied" as somebody who simply typed the wrong
             password.  The ssh server's log is the only place that says which
             it was.

14 Aug 26  The installer now tells you how accounts are really made.
           * Its closing message used to end with "net localgroup sdusers
             <name> /add", as though adding people to a group were the way to
             give them SD.  It is not, and it never made the Windows account.
           * It now leads with CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name>, which makes the
             Windows account and the SD account together and asks for the new
             password; says that this needs an elevated window, because
             creating a Windows user does; says that accounts made that way
             sign in over ssh only; and gives the ADMINISTRATOR keyword for an
             unrestricted one.
           * The net command is still there, as what it always was - the way to
             give SD to somebody who already has a Windows account.

14 Aug 26  The ssh-only restriction is confirmed working.
           * An account in "sdsshonly" was signed in over ssh, with a password
             and with a key, and ran a command - while being refused a local
             sign-in at the same moment.  So the restriction closes the screen
             in front of you without closing ssh, which is the whole point of
             it.
           * Remote Desktop is not yet confirmed.  The right that blocks it is
             applied, but nobody has watched it refuse a connection.
           * A NEW ACCOUNT MUST SIGN IN WITH ITS PASSWORD ONCE BEFORE AN SSH
             KEY WILL WORK.  This is how Windows behaves, not something SD
             chooses: an account that has never signed in has no profile
             folder, and ssh looks for authorized_keys inside it.  Sign in
             once with the password, then set up a key.

14 Aug 26  SD runs without "run as administrator", and starts by hand.
           * You no longer need an elevated window to use SD.  Being an
             administrator of the machine is what counts, not whether a
             particular window was started as one.
           * You DO have to sign out and back in once after installing, before
             SD can open its files.  The installer says so.  Until you do, it
             fails with "Error 13 allocating semaphores".
           * SD DOES NOT START AUTOMATICALLY when Windows starts.  Run
             "sd -start" after each restart.  Running SD as a Windows service
             is not done yet.
           * The installer's OpenSSH option may report that a restart is
             needed.  That means it worked - Windows registers the ssh service
             only after a reboot.  Restart, and ssh will be running.

14 Aug 26  Accounts SD creates can sign in over ssh only.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> now makes an account that can reach
             this machine ONLY over ssh.  It cannot sign in at the screen in
             front of you, and it cannot use Remote Desktop.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR is unchanged and is not
             restricted: administrators get an ordinary Windows account and
             can sign in locally as usual.
           * This works by putting the account in a new "sdsshonly" group.
             The installer denies that group the two local sign-in rights, so
             you can see who is restricted by looking at who is in the group.
             Removing someone from it lifts the restriction.
           * "sdusers" is unchanged and is a different thing - it grants
             access to the database files, and administrators are in it too.
           * Tested through ssh on 14 Aug 26 - see the entry above.

14 Aug 26  The installer's "install OpenSSH Server" option now works.
           * It never did.  The command it ran had a syntax error, so ticking
             the box installed nothing and reported nothing - no ssh server,
             no service, no message.  If you ticked it before and wondered why
             you had no ssh, that is why.
           * Be aware it can take several minutes.  Windows downloads OpenSSH
             on demand, and the installer cannot show progress while it does.
             It is not stuck.  Do not cancel it or end the Windows Modules
             Installer task, and expect to be asked to restart afterwards.

14 Aug 26  CREATE.ACCOUNT works on Windows, and can make an administrator.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> creates the Windows account as well
             as the SD one, prompts you for its password, and adds it to
             sdusers so it can reach the database.  It is a STANDARD local
             account.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER <name> ADMINISTRATOR makes it a Windows
             administrator instead, which is what makes it an SD
             administrator.  Leave the keyword off unless you mean it.
           * Run it from an elevated session.  Creating a Windows account
             needs administrator rights that an ordinary session does not
             carry, and without them it stops with an access denied error.
           * The CREATUSR configuration parameter is no longer consulted when
             creating an account.  SD has accounts, not accounts and users, so
             creating an account is creating the operating system account and
             there was nothing to opt in to.
           * Accounts could not be created at all before this: the check on
             the account pathname rejected every native Windows path, so the
             Windows account was created and then everything else stopped with
             "Invalid account pathname".

14 Aug 26  If you are a Windows administrator, you are an SD administrator.
           * There is no separate SD administrator group and nothing to join.
             Whoever installs SD administers it, because installing requires
             administrator rights in the first place.
           * It is your ACCOUNT that counts, not whether a particular window
             is "run as administrator".  Actions that genuinely need elevation
             - creating a Windows user among them - still do.
           * The installer no longer offers to set an SDSYS password.  It is
             not what makes you an administrator any more, and the step did
             not work: it needed a running server that the installer never
             started.  Accounts still have passwords; SET.PASSWORD still sets
             them.
           * "sdusers" is unchanged and is still needed.  It grants access to
             the database FILES, which is a different question from who
             administers SD - and an administrator who has not elevated needs
             it just as an ordinary user does.

14 Aug 26  The background daemon is now called sdwind, and it starts.
           * sdlnxd is now sdwind.  The old name meant "SD Linux daemon",
             which is wrong for a Windows system.  If you have scripts or
             monitoring that look for an sdlnxd process, change them.
           * IT WAS NOT RUNNING AT ALL on an installed system.  "sd -start"
             looked for it inside the database directory, where the Linux
             version used to keep the programs, and on Windows the programs
             are in C:\Program Files\SD instead - so it was never found.
             Nothing reported this: SD started, and everything you could type
             at it worked, because the daemon does none of that.
           * What you were missing is the clearing up of sessions that end
             without logging out - a machine switched off, or a connection
             dropped.  Those left entries behind that nothing removed.
           * "sd -start" now says so if the daemon cannot be started, instead
             of reporting success.

14 Aug 26  There is a Windows installer.
           * SD installs to C:\Program Files\SD and its database to
             C:\ProgramData\SD, and appears in Settings > Apps like any
             other program.
           * The database directory is locked down during installation, to
             SYSTEM, Administrators and a new "sdusers" group.  Before this it
             was readable by every account on the machine.
           * You are added to sdusers automatically, but WINDOWS ONLY APPLIES
             GROUP MEMBERSHIP WHEN YOU SIGN IN - so sign out and back in
             before using SD, or it will fail with "Error 13 allocating
             semaphores".  Add other users with
             "net localgroup sdusers <name> /add".
           * Optionally installs OpenSSH Server so people on your network can
             reach SD.  Off by default, and not offered if you already have
             one, which it will not touch.
           * Uninstalling does NOT remove your accounts, data or sd.conf.  It
             offers to, and the answer defaults to no.
           * Installing over an existing database leaves it alone; upgrading
             one in place is not yet supported.

14 Aug 26  SD finds its configuration file by itself.
           * It is %ProgramData%\SD\sd.conf, normally
             C:\ProgramData\SD\sd.conf, and nothing need be set in the
             environment for an installed system to start.
           * The environment variable that overrides it is SD_CONFIG, for the
             server and the client library alike.  The server used to read
             SCARLET_CONFIG and the client SD_CONFIG, so setting the one you
             would expect configured only one of them.  SCARLET_CONFIG IS NO
             LONGER READ - if you set it, set SD_CONFIG instead.
           * The server no longer falls back to /etc/sd.conf, and the client
             library no longer falls back to sd.ini in the Windows directory.
             Writing to C:\Windows has needed administrator rights since
             Vista.

14 Aug 26  "sd -stop" no longer kills the program that ran it.
           * It sent SIGTERM to every entry in the user table without
             checking the process id was valid, and a process id of zero
             means "every process in my process group" - so stopping SD from
             a script or a terminal could terminate the script, the shell, or
             anything else running alongside it.  No error was reported.

14 Aug 26  An SD account can be installed anywhere, and the install no longer
           needs Python or a compiler on the target machine.
           * The system is now compiled when the install is built rather than
             when it is installed, so installing is a file copy.
           * New build scripts gplbld/bootstrap.py and
             "gplbld/stage.py --bootstrap".

14 Aug 26  Configuration file fixes.
           * A CRLF sd.conf no longer corrupts every text setting.  The
             carriage return was left on the end of the value, so SDSYS
             pointed at a directory that did not exist.  Files written by
             Notepad and most Windows editors were affected; numeric settings
             were not.
           * SH and SH1 may be up to 255 characters, was 80.  A longer value
             used to overwrite the settings stored after them, and the error
             named SORTMRG - a parameter the file need not even contain.
             Over-long values are now refused with a message that names the
             right setting.

14 Aug 26  SH, ! and OS.EXECUTE now run PowerShell instead of bash.
           * SD used to shell out through /bin/bash, which is not present on
             a Windows machine and is not part of an SD install, so anything
             that shelled out worked only where MSYS2 happened to be
             installed.  PowerShell ships with Windows, so this works
             everywhere.
           * Commands passed to OS.EXECUTE are PowerShell, not shell script.
             Existing programs that shell out will need their command text
             changed.
           * SH and SH1 in sd.conf name the shell and can be set to something
             else.  The path must not contain spaces.

14 Aug 26  Creating an SD account creates a Windows account again, as it
           created a Linux one before.  Deleting one deletes it.
           * CREATE.ACCOUNT USER makes the Windows local user, adds it to
             sdusers, and creates the account's own local group.  It asks for
             the new user's Windows password itself and sets it; the account
             is created disabled and is enabled only once the password is
             set, so it is never briefly usable with no password.
           * DELETE.ACCOUNT offers to delete the Windows user, and removes
             the account's group.  The user's profile directory under
             C:\Users is left alone.
           * MODIFY.ACCOUNT ADD and DELETE add and remove Windows local group
             members.
           * THESE NEED AN ELEVATED SESSION.  Windows will not let an
             ordinary process create a user or change a local group, so
             account creation reports "OS Error: 5" unless SD was started
             elevated.  Everything that does not touch a Windows account
             works normally either way.
           * The Windows password is never placed on a command line, where
             any local user could read it.
           * Group membership is read from Windows directly.  It used to be
             read from /etc/group, which does not exist on this platform, so
             every membership test answered "no" - which, among other things,
             refused every login with "This user is not registered for SD
             use".

14 Aug 26  Pathnames containing spaces and backslashes are accepted where a
           pathname is checked before being passed to the operating system,
           so C:\Program Files\... no longer fails validation.

13 Aug 26  Embedded Python is removed.  SD for Windows is a back end data
           store reached through the API, and the interpreter was not part of
           that.
           * The SDEXT python keys, the SDPYOBJ function, and the PY_*
             subroutines in GPL.BP are all gone, along with SYSCOM/SDPYFUNC.H
             and the PY_* examples in the SDSYS BP file.  A program calling
             any of them will no longer compile.
           * Error codes -12001 to -12036 no longer exist.
           * The SDPYOBJ opcode is retired rather than reused, so existing
             compiled programs are unaffected unless they called it.
           * An installed system is about 40% smaller and needs three fewer
             support libraries.  Building no longer needs python-devel or
             gettext-devel.

13 Aug 26  An installed system no longer contains a copy of the C source, and
           does not need one to compile BASIC.
           * GPL.BP/APISRVR and GPL.BP/ERRTEXT each ran a build tool during
             compilation - REVSTAMP and ERRGEN - which read ./gplsrc from the
             account directory.  Both are now switched off, as CPROC's already
             was.  Compiling either program on a system without the C source
             used to fail, and in ERRGEN's case it emptied SYSCOM/ERR.H first.
           * New build script gplbld/gen_includes.py regenerates GPL.BP/
             REVSTAMP.H, GPL.BP/ERRTEXT.H and SYSCOM/ERR.H from the C headers.
             Run it after changing gplsrc/err.h or gplsrc/revstamp.h;
             "--check" reports what is out of date without writing anything.
           * 42 error codes had no message text - the crypto, SDEXT and
             embedded Python errors added since July 2024.  They have text
             now, so ERRTEXT and error displays name them instead of showing
             a bare number.
           * Those same 42 codes were defined in SYSCOM/ERR.H with the wrong
             sign and with C spelling.  They now match the C header:
             SD$Mem.Err is -10100, not SD_Mem_Err 10100.  No shipped program
             referred to them.
           * GPL.BP/OPGEN still reads ./gplsrc and so cannot be run on an
             installed system.  It is a developer tool for regenerating
             OPCODES.H and nothing calls it.

13 Aug 26  Administrator rights belong to the SDSYS account and to nothing
           else.
           * They are set on entry to SDSYS and given up on the way out, so
             they no longer follow a session into whatever account it moves
             to next, and being in a Windows group no longer confers them.
           * Only a program compiled $internal may set them, and only SDSYS
             may compile one.  Before this, any account could compile a three
             line program that made itself an administrator.
           * The no-password entry an administrator used to get for running an
             internal command is gone.
           * "sd" with no account named asks which account you want, instead
             of putting members of a Windows group straight into SDSYS.

13 Aug 26  Identity: accounts have their own passwords, and SD no longer
           consults operating system users or groups at all.
           * Every account carries a password.  Entry is by prompt, whether
             from "sd -Aname" at the shell or LOGTO inside SD.
           * New $CRED register in SDSYS holds a salt and an Argon2 verifier
             per account.  The password itself is never stored.
           * New verb SET.PASSWORD [account] sets one.
           * New ACCOUNTS field 4, ACC$USERS: the list of accounts allowed to
             LOGTO into that account.  Grants are recorded on the account
             being entered, so withdrawing one is a single edit.  Visible as
             "Granted to" in LIST ACCOUNTS, editable with MODIFY ACCOUNTS.
           * LOGTO SDSYS asks for your own password again, not an SDSYS one.
           * SDSYS reaches every account without exception.
           * LOGTO takes a registered account name only.  It used to accept a
             pathname, which reached an account directory without consulting
             its grant list.  APISRVR's account switch is name-only for the
             same reason.
           * @LOGNAME does not change on LOGTO, so what is done in another
             account stays attributed to whoever logged in.
           * The sdusers group test at login and the ACC$GROUP group test on
             LOGTO are gone, along with the sdadmins group requirement for
             ordinary use.  Knowing the SDSYS password is what confers
             administrator rights now.
           * New messages 10030 and 10031.

13 Aug 26  Accounts move to C:\ProgramData\SD\user_accounts and
           C:\ProgramData\SD\group_accounts (USRDIR and GRPDIR in sd.conf).
           /home/sd made sense while an SD account was an operating system
           user, which it no longer is.

13 Aug 26  Paths may now be written the Windows way.  A drive letter is
           understood as an absolute root and a backslash as a separator, so
           C:\ProgramData\SD, C:/ProgramData/SD and /c/ProgramData/SD all
           reach the same place (sdrealpath in linuxlb.c).  Every drive-letter
           spelling previously failed with "file not found".

13 Aug 26  Windows build.  Shared memory and semaphores use the POSIX calls
           rather than System V, which Windows does not have.  The client
           library is a native Win32 DLL and needs no MSYS2 runtime.

13 Aug 26  No binaries are kept in the repository.  Everything is built from
           source, so installing means building.

